HAN BHAGAT) \n \n(a) Progress in implementation of Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest \nDwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) Act, 2006 is furnished by the States on a monthly \nbasis indicating, the individual & community claims received, titles distributed and the \nextent of forest land for which titles have been distributed upto the end of that mont h. \nReports received from the states are compiled in the Ministry of Tribal Affairs and also \nput in public domain.  \n \n(b) No, Madam. \n \n(c) Does not arise in view of reply given to Part (b) of the question. \n \n**** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |302", "t": "Expansion of Natural Gas Pipelines", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 302 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.12.2017 \n \nEXPANSION OF NATURAL GAS PIPELINES  \n \n302.     SHRI BAIJAYANT JAY PANDA:    \n      \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \nisVªksfy;e vkSj izkd`frd xSl ea=h  \n \n(a) whether the Petroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board has grante d permission to GAIL \nfor laying and expanding natural gas pipeline along the Surat -Paradeep route and if so, the \ndetails and the current status thereof; \n \n \n(b) the status of the Expression of interest made by H -Energy Private Limited for laying and \noperating natural gas pipeline on the Contai-Paradeep-Dattapulia route; \n \n \n(c) whether the Government is aware that lack of  availability of natural gas pipeline \ninfrastructure in Odisha is adversely affecting the ability to establish CNG stations as a meas ure \nagainst vehicular pollution and if so, the details thereof and the reaction of the Government \nthereto; and \n \n(d) the concrete steps taken by the Government to ensure the availability of natural gas, pipeline \ninfrastructure and establishment of CNG stations in Odisha in a time-bound manner?  \n \nANSWER \n \nपेट्रोलियम और प्राकृतिक गैस मंत्री (श्री धमेन्‍द र प्रधान) \n \nMINISTER OF PETROLEUM & NATURAL GAS (SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a): Petroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board (PNGRB) granted authorization in the year \n2012 to GAIL to lay, built and operate or expand natural gas pipeline along the Surat -Paradeep \nroute. This pipeline will cover the State s of Gujarat, Maharashtra, Chattis garh and Odisha.  \nSubsequent to the authorization, GAIL has completed the route survey work  for section \nParadip/Dhamra to Deori of 1000 KM in December, 2016. However, due to lack of firm tie -ups \nwith anchor load customers en-route pipeline, the project activities are kept on hold.  \n \n(b): Based on expression of interest (EOI) made by H -Energy Private Limited (HEPL) to \nPNGRB, a tender to grant authorization for developing Contai  - Dattapulia – Jajpur – Dhamra \n– Cuttack -Paradip Natural Gas Pipeline was invite d by PNGRB in October, 2015. However, \nthe PNGRB has annulled the entire bidding process in July, 2016.  \n \n(c) & (d): In order to make available clean and eco -friendly fuel, i.e. natural gas to the eastern \nparts of the country, the Government has taken a de cision to provide a capital grant of Rs.5176 \ncrore (i.e. 40% of the estimated capital cost of Rs.12,940 Crore) to GAIL for development of a \n2655 Km long Jagdishpur -Haldia/Bokaro-Dhamra Gas Pipeline (JHBDPL) project, popularly \n\nknown as the “Pradhan Mantri U rja Ganga” of Eastern India in a time bound manner. This \npipeline will transport Natural Gas to the industrial, commercial, domestic and transport sectors \nin the States of Bihar, Jharkhand, Odisha, West Bengal and Uttar Pradesh. In the state of \nOdisha, the pipeline is being constructed of about 762 km covering 13 districts, i.e., Bhadrak, \nJajpur, Dhenkanal, Angul, Sundergarh, Sambalpur, Jharsuguda, Debagarh, Jagatsinghpur, \nCuttack, Khordha, Puri and Kendrapara. It will connect major industrial clusters of K hurdha, \nJharsuguda, Rourkela, Sambalpur, Bhubaneswar, Cuttack, Angul, Dhenkanal, Kalinganagar, \nJajpur and Paradip. Further,  the government has also approved the development of City Gas \nDistribution (CGD) network including Compressed Natural Gas (CNG) stat ions in \nBhubaneswar and Cuttack in synchronization of JHBDPL project in Odisha. In the city of \nBhubneshwar, Piped Natural Gas (PNG) supply to households has been commenced in October, \n2017.  CNG dispensing services in Bhubneshwar is also likely to commence soon. \n \n \nPNGRB has identified 10 Geographical Areas (GAs) in Odisha depending on the natural gas \npipeline connectivity/natural gas availability and techno -commercial feasibility for grant of \nauthorization to develop CGD network. \n \n \n \n \n \n***** \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |303", "t": "Accidents at Steel Plants", "head": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.303 \nFOR ANSWER ON 18/12/2017 \n \nACCIDENTS AT STEEL PLANTS \n \n303. SHRI CHANDRAKANT KHAIRE: \n \nWill the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the number of  accidents occurred in the integrated steel  plants \nboth in public and private sectors during the last three years, plant-wise; \n \n(b) the number of workers both regular  and under contract died and injured in \nthose accidents during the said period, plant-wise; and \n \n(c) the details of compensation paid to the affected workers/families? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR STEEL         (SHRI VISHNU DEO SAI) \n \n(a) A statement showing the plant -wise and year -wise number of accidents, both \nfatal and non -fatal in the public sector integrated steel plants of Steel Authority of \nIndia Limited (SAIL) and Rashtriya Ispat Nigam Limited (RINL) during the last three \nyears is at Annexure I. \n \nSteel is a deregulated sector. There are a large number of steel \nfactories/plants in the country. As regards the private sector integrated steel plants, \nthe requisite data/information is not maintained by the Ministry of Steel. \n \n(b) A statem ent showing the plant -wise and year -wise number of workers both \nregular and under contract died and injured in accidents in the integrated steel plants \nof Steel Authority of India Limited (SAIL) and Rashtriya Ispat Nigam Limited (RINL) \nduring the last three years is at Annexure II. \n \n(c) In case of fatal accidents of regular employees, the compensation is given as \nper the law/company policy. SAIL and RINL provide compensation to their employees \nin case of death/disablement due to accident arising out of and in course of \nemployment as per The Employees’ Compensation Act, Employee Family Benefit \nScheme and company policy. In case of contract labour, compensation/dependent \nbenefit is paid under the Employee State Insurance Scheme (ESIS) by the ESI \nCorporation. S AIL and RINL have paid approximately Rs.5.35 crores as \ncompensation to the injured persons and families of deceased during 2014-2016. \n \n******** \n  \n\nAnnexure-I  \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.303 FOR ANSWER ON 18.12.2017 BY SHRI \nCHANDRAKANT KHAIRE  \n \nPlant/ Unit 2014 2015 2016 \nSteel Authority of India Limited \nBhilai Steel Plant(Chhattisgarh) 14 16 6 \nDurgapur Steel Plant(West Bengal) 5 3 1 \nRourkela Steel Plant(Odisha) 3 7 5 \nBokaro Steel Plant (Jharkhand) 10 8 5 \nIISCO Steel Plant(West Bengal) 14 8 2 \nTotal (SAIL) 46 42 19 \nRashtriya Ispat Nigam Limited (RINL) 18 17 15 \nGRAND TOTAL 64 59 34 \n \n \nAnnexure-II  \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.303 FOR ANSWER ON 18.12.2017 BY SHRI \nCHANDRAKANT KHAIRE \nStatement showing details of number of workers (both regular and under \ncontract) died and injured in Public Sector Integrated Steel Plants of Steel \nAuthority of India Limited (SAIL) and Rashtriya Ispat Nigam Limited (R INL) \nduring the last three years (plant-wise)  \n \nPlants / Units No. of persons died No. of persons injured \nPeriod 2014 2015 2016 2014 2015 2016 \nSTEEL AUTHORITY OF INDIA LIMITED (SAIL) \nBhilai Steel Plant (Chhattisgarh) 8 3 3 11 13 3 \nDurgapur Steel Plant(West Bengal) 4 5 1 2 2 0 \nRourkela Steel Plant(Odisha) 2 6 2 1 2 3 \nBokaro Steel Plant(Jharkhand) 2 1 2 8 7 3 \nIISCO Steel Plant(West Bengal) 8 0 1 29 8 1 \nTotal (SAIL) 24 15 09 51 32 10 \nRashtriya Ispat Nigam Ltd. 05 04 06 14 13 10 \nGRAND TOTAL 29 19 15 65 45 20 \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |304", "t": "Financial Assistance to NGOs for Tribal Welfare", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †304 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.12.2017 \n \nFINANCIAL ASSISTANCE TO NGOS FOR TRIBAL WELFARE \n \n†304. SHRIMATI KAMLA DEVI PAATLE:  \n \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of financial assistance provided to NGOs/voluntary organizations by the \nGovernment for the welfare of tribals in the country during each year of the last three years and \nthe current year, State/UT-wise; \n \n(b) whether the cases of misappropriation of such funds by the non-governmental organizations or \nvoluntary organizations have come to the notice of the Government and if so, the details thereof, \nState/UT-wise and the action taken by the Government against such organizations; and \n \n(c) the steps taken/being taken to ensure appropriate utilization of such funds by the above \norganizations? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SHRI JASWANTSINH BHABHOR) \n \n(a): The details, State-wise, of financial assistance provided by the Ministry of Tribal Affairs \nto NGOs/VOs working for the welfare of tribals in the country during the last three years and the \ncurrent financial year under the schemes being implemented by the Ministry are annexed. \n \n(b): No such instance of misappropriation of funds by NGOs has come to the notice of the \nMinistry. \n \n(c): To ensure proper utilization of funds by the NGOs  / VOs, the projects/schemes, are \nmonitored through:  \n \ni. Mandatory annual inspection by District Authorities.  \nii. Scrutiny of proposals by multidisciplinary State Level Committee every Year.  \niii. Mandatory submission of Audited statements of accounts as well as Utilization \nCertificate pertaining to the releases.  \niv. Field inspections by Officers from State Government / Central Government. \n \nIn addition to the above, the accounts of grantee institutions are open for inspection by \nrepresentatives/officers from the office of Comptroller and Auditor General of India, or concerned \nState Govt. at any time. \n \n***** \n\nAnnex \n \nAnnex referred to reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 304 for 18.12.2017 raised \nby Shrimati Kamla Devi Paatle regarding “Financial Assistance to NGOs for Tribal Welfare” \n \nDetails of Funds released under the Scheme of   Grant- in-Aid to Vountary organisations \nworking for the Welfare of STs. \n(Amount in Rs) \nSl.No. Name of the State 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 2017-18(as on \n08.12.2017) \n1 Andhra Pradesh 3443334 25263164 16058361 2256057 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 44975364 9569894 45605089 12676140 \n3 Assam 8755425 5785170 13766060 8761477 \n4 Chhattisgarh 4943900 1620270 6596669 2396860 \n5 Gujarat 21372227 4338188 11193835 1879050 \n6 Himachal Pradesh 17074044 0 27733860 5687777 \n7 Jharkhand 65726048 20519422 40575895 33454643 \n8 Jammu & Kashmir 4035911 0 0 1968601 \n9 Karnataka 24888744 22725348 38909904 8765995 \n10 Kerala 9907370 9625460 11355052 3671288 \n11 Madhya Pradesh 10189512 6654020 15278404 2065600 \n12 Maharashtra 19697375 3377213 20295458 20185061 \n13 Manipur 21091546 6346350 39406207 3715470 \n14 Meghalaya 67215218 27479208 60692770 31203557 \n15 Mizoram 4039560 0 4016475 3810494 \n16 Nagaland 3215437 0 0 0 \n17 Odisha 30398337 19079568 45737410 26222733 \n18 Rajasthan 8331000 3182749 6783268 1449967 \n19 Sikkim 2564384 5454113 5205330 4787541 \n20 Tamil Nadu 3509519 1181790 3891019 3928965 \n21 Telangana 2375010 1976326 63786629 0 \n22 Tripura 1715310 1582470 6602040 0 \n23 Uttrakhand 11866736 1766858 11263873 2907065 \n24 Uttar Pradesh 6904581 2218403 3448897 1582470 \n25 West Begnal 45252818 37791760 31533450 49380580 \n26 Delhi 1511290 0 907171 895719 \n  Grand Total 445000000 217537744 530643126 233653110 \n \n  \n\nDetails of Funds released under the Scheme of Strengthening Education among ST Girls in Low \nLiteracy Districts \n(Amount in Rs) \nS.No. State 2014-15   2015-16   2016-17   2017-18  (as on \n08.12.2017)                                   \n1 Andhra Pradesh 5207920 193107731 27094085 0 \n2 Arunachal \nPradesh 4271709 0 0 1940050 \n3 Chhattisgarh 3925448 0 3963499 0 \n4 Gujarat 194219225 171232411 285792943 1706505 \n5 Jharkhand 4442520 2107200 0 0 \n6 Madhya Pradesh 57103228 14357845 42004939 0 \n7 Maharashtra 3600400 0 15838410 3652400 \n8 Odisha 26507661 104081259 192941396 61115701 \n9 Rajasthan 9665119 0 14877810 2513350 \n10 Telangana 41056770 48075810 71975792   \nTotal  350000000 532962256 654488874 70928006 \n \n \nDetails of Funds released under the Scheme of Vocational Training in Tribal Areas. \n(Amount in Rs) \nSl.No. Name of the State \n2014-15   2015-16   2016-17 2017-18(as on \n08.12.2017) \n1 Assam 7232000 0 9300000 9240000 \n2 Karnataka 6360000 0 0 3000000 \n3 Madhya Pradesh 0 0 0 5793259 \n4 Meghalaya 3044800 0 0 3112000 \n5 Nagaland 10392000 0 2448000 0 \n6 Tamil Nadu 0 0 3120000 3120000 \n  Grand Total 27028800 0 14868000 24265259 \n \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |305", "t": "Out of School Students", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 305\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH DECEMBER, 2017\nOut of School Students\n305. SHRI R. DHRUVA NARAYANA:\nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether 2.8 million children are out of school in the country as per the UNESCO  \nreport;\n(b) if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise; and\n(c)  whether  the  Government  has  taken  note  of  it  and  if  so,  the  details  of  the \ncomprehensive  measures  taken  by  the  Government  to  bring  these  children  to \nschool?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI UPENDRA KUSHWAHA)\n(a): Yes, Madam. As per UNESCO’s Global Education Monitoring Report, 2017-18,  \n2.8 million children were estimated to be out of school in India.\n(b): It has been informed by UNESCO that they collect data only at the national level. \nHence, State/UT-wise details are not available.\n(c): The Centrally Sponsored Scheme of Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA) provides  \nassistance to State Governments and UTs to ensure free and compulsory quality  \nelementary education for all children. Universal enrolment is the first goal of SSA,  \nand hence all efforts are made to bring all children to schools. SSA has provided  \n2.06  lakh  primary  and  1.61  lakh  upper  primary  schools  for  ensuring  universal \nprovisioning of schooling facilities. Preference for opening of school is given to tribal  \nareas, and areas with high concentration of Scheduled Caste, Scheduled Tribe and  \nminority population. In addition, 3703 Kasturba Gandhi Balika Vidyalayas, which are  \nresidential schools for girls at upper primary level, have also been sanctioned to the  \nStates for improving access to marginalised girls particularly out of school, either  \ndrop out or never enrolled. As per Unified District Information System for Education  \n\n2015-16, the number of total children enrolled at elementary level has increased  \nfrom 15.1 crore in 1998-99 to 19.67 crore.\nUnder SSA, provisions have also been made for residential schools/hostels and  \ntransportation/escort facility for children living in sparsely populated areas, children  \nliving in areas where schools cannot be opened due to unavailability of land and  \nchildren in need of care and protection. Special training is also provided to all such  \nchildren who are school dropouts and long absentees and they are enrolled in “back  \nto school” camps.\nAdditionally,  other  strategies  adopted  under  SSA such  as  strengthening  school \ninfrastructure, improving pupil-teacher ratios, providing incentives like free textbooks,  \nuniforms for eligible category of children and mid-day meal in schools have proved to \nbe useful in increasing the enrolment of children in elementary schools.\n*******"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |306", "t": "Biannual NEET and JEE", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 306 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18th DECEMBER 2017 \nAGRAHAYANA 27, 1939 (SAKA)  \n \nBiannual NEET and JEE. \n \n306.      SHRI BHAGWANT MANN: \n           \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has any proposal to conduct medical and engineering \n admission exams, National Eligibility cum Entrance Test (NEET) and Joint \n Entrance Examination (JEE) twice a year to reduce pressure on students; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c)  the way students are likely to be benefited by the said pattern? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI UPENDRA KUSHWAHA) \n \n(a) to (c): The Government has decided to setup a National Testing Agency (NTA) \nas a Society registered under the Indian Societies Registration Act, 1860, and as an \nautonomous and self -sustained premier testing organization to conduct all entrance \nexaminations for higher educational institutions.  The features for the said examinations \nare (i) the NTA would initially conduct those entrance examinations which are curren tly \nbeing conducted by the CBSE; (ii) Other examinations will be taken up gradually after \nNTA is fully geared up; (iii) The entrance examinations will be conducted in online mode \nat least twice a year, thereby giving adequate opportunity to can didates to bring out \ntheir best; and (iv) In order to serve the requirements of the ru ral students, it would \nlocate the centres at sub -district/district level and as far as possible would undertake \nhands-on training to the students. \n*** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |307", "t": "Labour Conference", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 307 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.12.2017 \n \nLABOUR CONFERENCE \n307. SHRI G. HARI: \n  Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state:  \n(a)whether the Government has finalised the agenda for the 47th Indian \nLabour Conference;  \n(b)if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)whether providing social security cover for the entire workforce may \nbe the central theme of the 47th Indian Labour Conference and if  so, \nthe details thereof;  \n(d)whether the Government has received comments/suggestions from all \nstakeholders for finalising the agenda of Indian Labour Conference; \nand  \n(e)if so, the details thereof?  \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI SANTOSH KUMAR GANGWAR) \n \n(a)  to (c): Yes, Madam. The agenda of the 47 th Indian Labour \nConference was decided in the 48 th Session of Standing Labour \nCommittee held on 11 th May, 2017 in New Delhi and the details of \nagenda are as under: \n \n i.     Social Security for all. \n ii.    Labour law amendment, reforms and codification \n iii.   Employment Generation with special focus on women \n iv.   Transition to formal employment.  \n  \n(d) & (e):  The agenda of Indian Labour Conference is finalised by the \ntripartite stakeholders after a thorough deliberations in Standing Labour \nCommittee as indicated above.  \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |308", "t": "GST Literacy under PMKVY", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 308 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18.12.2017 \n \nGST LITERACY UNDER PMKVY \n \n \n308. SHRI RAVNEET SINGH \n \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP \nbe pleased to state : \n  \n(a) whether any support has been provided under the  Pradhan  Mantri Kaushal \nVikas Yojana (PMKVY) to create a GST literate workf orce that can assist smaller \nenterprises for their GST filings and if so, the details thereof; and \n   \n(b) the details of the curriculum of the courses, n umber of people trained and \nduration of the course, State-wise ? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI ANANTKUMAR HEGDE) \n \n(a) and (b) Ministry of Skill Development and Entre preneurship, in consultation \nwith Department of Revenue, Central Board  of Excis e & Custom (CBEC), and \nNational Academy of Customs, Indirect Taxes & Narco tics (NACIN) under  Ministry \nof Finance, had finalized a roadmap to execute a tr aining program for GST under \nMSDE’s flagship program Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vika s Yojana (PMKVY). The \ncandidates trained under this program provide suppo rt to small and medium \nenterprises/entrepreneurs for filing their returns under GST. \n \nTo execute this training, Banking Financial Service s and Insurance Sector \nSkill Council (BFSI SSC) under the supervision of M SDE, developed a 140 hour \n(including 40 hours of digital literacy) course cal led the GST Accounts Assistant \nTraining. The job role is a level 4 course as per N ational Skill Qualification \nFramework (NSQF) and is duly validated by industry experts. Under PMKVY (2016-\n20), 4375 candidates have been trained under GST. The State-wise details are given \nat Annexure . \n  \n\nAnnexure \n \nState-wise number of candidates trained under GST A ccounts Assistant job \nrole under PMKVY 2016-20: \n \nS. No. State Name Total Trained \n1.  Andhra Pradesh 150 \n2.  Assam 150 \n3.  Bihar 449 \n4.  Chhattisgarh 89 \n5.  Delhi 150 \n6.  Gujarat 0 \n7.  Haryana 179 \n8.  Jammu and Kashmir 84 \n9.  Jharkhand 0 \n10.  Karnataka 436 \n11.  Kerala 150 \n12.  Madhya Pradesh 277 \n13.  Maharashtra 45 \n14.  Odisha 283 \n15.  Punjab 60 \n16.  Rajasthan 141 \n17.  Tamil Nadu 0 \n18.  Telangana 120 \n19.  Tripura 90 \n20.  Uttar Pradesh 1222 \n21.  Uttarakhand 0 \n22.  West Bengal 300 \nTotal  4375  \n \n****** \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |309", "t": "Differently Abled Children", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 309\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 18TH DECEMBER, 2017\nDifferently Abled Children\n309. SHRI B. SENGUTTUVAN:\nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether it is true that the  attendance of the differently abled children in the age  \ngroup of 5-19 years at school has gone up and if so, the details thereof; and\n(b) the percentage of the differently abled children attending schools in the country  \nduring the last three years, State/UT-wise?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI UPENDRA KUSHWAHA)\n(a): Attendance record of the differently abled children is not maintained Centrally.  \nHowever,  enrolment of children with  special  needs  (CWSN) at elementary level \n(Class I to VIII) has decreased from 25.03 lakh in 2013-14 to 22.86 lakh in 2015-16.  \nSimilarly, at the secondary (Class IX – X) and higher secondary level (Class XI – XII)  \nthe number of CWSN has gone down from 2.88 lakh in 2013-14 to 2.79 lakh in 2015-\n16.\n(b): State/UT-wise details of the percentage of enrolment of differently abled children  \nattending schools in the country during the last three years as per Unified District  \nInformation System for Education are at Annexure-I & II.\n*******\n\nANNEXURE-I\nANNEXURE  REFERRED  TO  IN  REPLY  TO  PART  (b)  OF  LOK  SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 30