chal Pradesh Crop Diversification Promotion \nProject (IDP 213)\nGovt of Japan\nID-P 213\n17.2.2011\n15.06.2021\nHimachal Pradesh\n5001.00\n3560.54\n22\nGreen-Agriculture, Transforming Indian agriculture \nfor global environmental benefits (PPG)\nFAO\nGCP/IND/182/\nGFF\n16.08.2016\n31.12.2017\nOdisha, Madhya Pradesh, \nUttarakhand, Rajasthan, \nMizoram)\n0.30\n0.243\n23\nTechnical Assistance for Farmer Water School \nProgramme and Agricultural Activities Under \nUPWSRP II\nFAO\nUTF/IND/195/I\nND\n30.10.2020\nUttar Pradesh\n1.85\n0.6\n24\nDevelopment of Extension and Outreach \norganizational and managerial capacities by State \nand Public institutions in Mizoram FAO’s Regular \nProgramme Resources(TCP)\nFAO\nTCP/IND/3501\n31.12.2017\nMizoram\n0.49\n0.4\n25\nStrengthening National Forest inventory and \nMonitoring Protocols and Capacities in India \nFAO\nTCP/IND/3503\n31.12.2017\nPunjab, Madhya Pradesh, West \nBengal, Uttarakhand, \nMeghalaya Karnataka\n0.39\n0.23\n26\nTCPF: Strengthening insti"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |478", "t": "De-addiction Camps", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 478 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.12.2017 \n \nDE-ADDICTION CAMPS  \n \n478. DR. BOORA NARSAIAH GOUD: \n \nWill the Minister of SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether de-addiction camps are being organized by the Government regularly;  \n \n(b) if so, the details and the number of beneficiaries from such camps during each of the last three  years and the \ncurrent year;  \n \n(c) the status of the National Toll free Drug de-Addiction Helpline; and  \n \n(d) the details of the number of victims of drug addiction in the country during the said period? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR SOCIAL JUSTICE AND EMPOWERMENT \n(SHIR VIJAY SAMPLA) \n(a) & (b): This Ministry implements a “Central Sector Scheme of Assistance for Prevention of Alcoholism an d \nSubstance (Drug) Abuse”. The Scheme has the provision of financial assistance to Non -Governmental \nOrganisations, Panchayati Raj Institutions, Urban Local  Bodies etc. for, inter -alia, running and maintenance of \nIntegrated Rehabilitation Centre for Addicts (IRCAs) and to provide composite/ integrated services f or the \nrehabilitation of the addicts. These IRCAs are responsible for Preventive Education and Awar eness Generation \nprogrammes in their neighbourhood, educational institutions, workplace and slums with the purpose of  \nsensitising the community about the impact of addiction and the need to take professional help for t reatment. \nTotal number of beneficiarie s under the scheme for t he year 2014 -15,2015-16,2016-17& 2017 -18(upto \n13.12.2017) are 108855, 146124, 114759 & 65682 respectively. \n(c): This Ministry has setup a National Toll Free Drug  de-addiction Helpline No. 1800 -11-0031 w.e.f. 7 th \nJanuary, 2015 to hel p the victims of drug abuse, their family & society at large. The helpline has become \noperational  24x7 since March, 2017. \n(d): The last National Survey on the extent, pattern and trend of drug abuse was sponsored by Minist ry of Social \nJustice and Empowerment and the United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime (UNODC) in the year 2000 -2001 \nand its report was published in 2004. It was estimated that about 732 lakh persons in India were use rs of alcohol \nand drugs. Of these 87 lakh used Cannabis, 20 lakh used opi ates and 625 lakh were users of Alcohol. However, \nthe sample size being small (40,697 males within the age group of 12 -16 years both in rural and urban areas) \nvis-à-vis the country’s population, the estimates can at best be taken as indicative only. \n Thereafter, no National Survey on drug abuse was con ducted. However, the Ministry ha s, in the month \nof August, 2016, assigned the work of conducting the National Survey on Extent and Pattern of Substa nce Use \nin India to National Drug Dependence Treatment Centre  (NDDTC), All India Institute of Medi cal Sciences \n(AIIMS), New Delhi.  The survey will provide national and State -level estimates of proportion and absolute \nnumber of people who use various substances and people who are suffering from substance use disorders. \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |48", "t": "Converge of Schemes for Children", "head": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. *48 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19TH DECEMBER, 2017 \nUSE OF PUBLIC TRANSPORT \n \nNo. *48         PROF. CHINTAMANI MALVIYA \n  \n \n Will the Minister of Housing and Urban Affairs be pleased to \nstate: \n \na) whether the Government proposes to encourage citizens to use \ncity bus services instead of priv ate vehicles and auto rickshaws \nin the country; \nb) if so, the details thereof; \nc) if not, the reasons thereof; \nd) whether the Government proposes to issue directions to the \nGovernment and private employees for using public \ntransport/buses run by the Government, metro services etc; and \ne) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE  MINISTRY \nOF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) to (e): A Statement is la id on the Table of the House. \n***** \n \n\n  \n\n-2- \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (e) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *48 FOR ANSWER ON 19 TH \nDECEMBER, 2017 REGARDING “USE OF PUBLIC TRANSPORT” ASKED \nBY PROF. CHINTAMANI MALVIYA.  \n \n(a)to(c): Urban Transport is intertwined with Urban Development which \nis a State subject. Therefore, the responsibility for promoting \npublic transport including city bus service rests with the \nrespective States/Union Territori es. Government of India has \nissued National Urban Tran sport Policy, 2006 which \nencourages use of public transport.  \n(d)to(e): No, Madam. Citizens hav e the discretion for choosing the \nmode of transport for themselves. \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |480", "t": "City Compost", "head": "     \n   GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \n \n   UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 480 \n                         TO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 19, 2017 \n \n                                                                                                                                                                         CITY COMPOST  \n \n \n \nNo. 480            SHRIMATI KIRRON KHER: \n      \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AN D URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate: \n \n \n(a) whether the policy for promot ion of city compost has been \nsuccessful in creating a market  for compost and increasing compost \nconsumption in the country and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) the details of employment oppo rtunities generated by city waste \ncomposting in urban areas; and \n \n(c) whether the Government has take n steps to regulate production of \ncompost according to the amount of compost cons umed by the market \nand if so, the details thereof? \n \n \n   \n       ANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE MINISTRY \nOF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a): Yes, Madam. T he Policy on Promotion of City Compost has been \nsuccessful in creating a market  for compost and increasing the \ncompost consumption.  The production of compost has increased by \n18% and its sale has increased by 29% since the launch of  policy in \nJanuary, 2016.  \n \n(b): Employment opportunities hav e been generated from city waste \ncomposting in urban areas, including the involvement of Self Help \nGroups (SHGs) in the collection and processing of waste. Precise \ndetails of employment formation in the informal sector are not \nmaintained.  \n \n(c): Government does not regulat e production of compost according to \nthe amount of compost consumed by the market. \n \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |481", "t": "Pending Bill", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 481 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH DECEMBER, 2017 /AGRAHAYANA 28, 1939 (SAKA) \n \nPENDING BILL \n \n481.  SHRI JAYADEV GALLA: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether it is true that the  Legislative Assembly of Andhra Pradesh  has \npassed The Andhra Pradesh Money  Lenders Bill, 2016 and sent it to the  \nGovernment for approval in 2016; and \n \n(b) if so, the reasons why the Bill is  still pending with the Government and the \ntime by which the Bill is likely to be approved? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n(a) : Yes, Madam.  \n(b) : The Andhra Pradesh Money Lenders Bill, 2016 was received in the Ministry \nof Home Affairs  on 20.01.2016 for the consideration of the Hon’ble President of \nIndia under Article 201 of the Constitution of India. This Bill has been returned to \nthe State Government on their request on 20.04.2017. \n****** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |482", "t": "Corruption Cases in PSUs", "head": "Annexure-A \nS. \nNo \nSubject Status of the complaint \n \n1 Complaint of corruption in IFFCO ref. \nreceived from Sh. Nishikant Dubey, \nMP (LS) through CVC. \nComplaint has been forwarded to CBI, \nED for investigation. Report is awaited. \n2 RCs against Shri Mahesh Mittal, \nDirector M/s Narmada Extension, \nIndore, against RCF - Complaint \nreceived from CVC pertaining to filing \nof chargesheet by CBI against Sh. \nMahesh Mittal and recommendation of \nRDA by CBI against officials of RCF.  \nCase in under investigation in \nconsultation with CVC and CVO, RCF. \n3. Irregularities in NFL’s contract for \nproduction of Urea and Ammonia. \nRef. received from CVC. \nReport has been sent to CVC and \nadvice of the Commission is awaited.  \n4. Complaint against Sh. Jaiveer \nSrivastava, Ex -CMD, FACT