nsultation with CMD, FACT. \n13. Complaint against Ex -CMD, NFL and \nother officials of NFL raising several \nallegations such as irregu alarities in \nAGM Meeting in NFL, procurement of \nHDPE bags by NFL, promotons etc.: \nRef. received from CVC \nAll allegations have been closed in \nconsultation with CVC except \nallegation pertaining to the \nprocurement of HDPE bags which has \nbeen referred to CBI for detailed \ninvestigation . Report from CBI is \nawaited. \n14. Procurment of SSP on long term basis \nfrom M/ S Devyani Phosphates Pvt. \nLtd.by RCF. \nCase in under investigation in \nconsultation with CVC and CVO, RCF. \n15. Complaint against MFL officers in the \nmatter of procurement of Oil \nCompressors-Ref received from \nCVO,MFL \nCBI has filed Charge sheet in the \nSpecial court, Chennai. Copy of \nchargesheet furnished by CBI had \nbeen sent to the CVC on  22.11.2017  \nto seek its further advice in the matter \nwhich is awaited.  \n16. Complaint under public interest \ndisclosure per whistle Blower Policy \nMFL regarding procurment of  Primary \nReformer Tubes in MFL- Regarding. \nCBI has registered FIR in the matter. \nReport from CBI is awaited. \n17. Complaint against Sh. D. \nSundraShekran, GM, MFL- regarding \ntinkering of various tenders such as \nlabour contract, plant upkeep and \nmanagement, b agging contract in \norder to suit certain parties.  \nCharge Sheet  has been issued to  Dr. \nI. Vijayakumar Ex-CMD, MFL.  \n18. Regarding purchase of  Import \nDAP/NPK by RCF from Zuari Agro -\nChemicals Ltd.(ZACL) - Explanation \nsought by Hon’ble MOS(I/C),C&F) \nReport has been sent to CVC for 1 st \nstage advice. Advice of the CVC is \nawaited.  \n19. Appointment of wholesale dealers in \nNFL \n Chargehseet for Major Penalty \nProceedings has been served to Sh. P \nK Kaul, then Dir.(Mktg.), NFL. The \npenalty to be imposed on then CMD, \nNFL is under consideration of \nDepartment. \n20. Complaint against Sh. P.S. Ghalot, \nMD,IPL- Ref. from CVC \nCase in under investigation in \nconsultation with CVC. \n \n********** \n \n \n \n \n \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |483", "t": "Wastage of Foodgrains", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 483 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19TH DECEMBER, 2017 \n \nWASTAGE OF FOODGRAINS \n \n483.  SHRI PR. SENTHIL NATHAN: \nSHRI BHARATHI MOHAN R.K.: \nSHRI M. MURALI MOHAN: \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has taken serious note of wastage of \nfoodgrains due to poor storage, pilferage, theft, rotting,  decaying etc. and if \nso, the details thereof  and the decision taken by the Government  in this \nregard; \n \n(b)  whether the Government has taken steps to increase the overall \nstorage capacities of foodgrains in the country  keeping in view the need to \nprocure and store more foodgrains in the coming years  and if so, the details \nthereof and the total  volume of foodgrains in Government‘s storage till date \nalong with the details of the capacity increase made in the last three years; \n \n(c)  the quantum of foodgrains in terms  of bags found unfit for human  \nconsumption, exclusively rice and wheat location-wise and State-wise during \nthe said period; \n \n(d)  the remedial measures being undertaken by the Government to \nprevent accumulation of spoiled foodgrains  exclusively rice and wheat in \nvarious godowns in the entire country when millions of people are facing \nstarvation deaths; and \n \n(e)  the corrective measures being undertaken to prevent the lapses in \nthe process of storage to distribution of rice and wheat? \n \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR  CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI C. R. CHAUDHARY) \n(a) & (b) No damage of foodgrains have been reported due to lack of \nadequate storage and handling facilities  in Food Corporation of India (FCI). \nProcured foodgrains are stored in scientific godowns and code of scientific \nstorage practices are followed during storage. Inspite of all precautions, \nsome quantity of foodgrains may get damaged/become non- issuable during \nstorage due to various reasons such as storage pests’ attack, leakages in \ngodowns, exposure to rains, floods, etc. A quantity of 3115 tons and 8776 \ntons of foodgrai ns accrued as damaged/non- issuable in FCI godowns during \n2015-16 and 2016-17 respectively. \n...2/- \n\n...2... \n \n         Sufficient storage capacity is available for storage of Central Pool \nfoodgrains stocks. Storage capacity for Central Pool foodgrain is 728.83 lakh \ntons against the stock of 378.77 lakh tons as on 31.10.2017.  \n \n         Total volume of Central Pool  foodgrains is 347.91 lakh tons as on \n01.12.2017. Following steps have been taken by the Government to augment \ncovered storage capacity: \n \n1.  Government has formulated Private Entrepreneurs Guarantee (PEG) \nScheme for construction of godowns for storage of foodgrains by private \nparties, Central Warehouse Corporation (CWC), State Warehouse \nCorporations (SWCs) and other State agencies. Storage capacity of 139.72 \nlakh tons has been completed as on 31.10.2017.  \n \n2.  In addition, a capacity of 1,22,270 tons in North Eastern States and \n20,000 tons in other than NE States as on 30.11.2017 has been completed \nunder 12\nth Five Year Plan.  \n \n3.  Government has also approved action plan for construction of steel \nSilos for a capacity of 100 lakh  tons in phased manner. \n \n          Details of the capacity added during the last three years is as under: \n(fig in tons) \nYear Capacity under PEG Scheme Capacity under Plan Scheme \n2014-15 830000 43480 \n2015-16 646000 64810 \n2016-17 383000 3980 \nTotal 1859000 112270 \n \n         The remedial measures taken by the Government to prevent losses on \naccount of pilferage and theft are at Annexure-I.   \n \n(c)   Location wise/bag wise/State wise details of the foodgrains accrued \nas damaged/non-issuable in FCI during the last three years and current year \nare at Annexure-II.     \n \n(d) & (e) Steps taken by the Government to avoid the damage in storage to \ndistribution of foodgrains are at Annexure-III. \n**** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nANNEXURE-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART ( a) & (b) OF UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO 483 TO BE ANSWERED ON 19 TH DECEMBER, 2017 IN LOK \nSABHA. \nREMEDIAL MEASURES TAKEN BY THE GOVERNMENT TO PREVENT LOSSES \nON ACCOUNT OF PILFERAGE AND THEFT. \nFollowing measures were taken by FCI to avoid foodgrains losses on account of \npilferage and theft.   \n1. Installation of barbed wires fencing of boundary walls and provision of high mast \nlights in the godowns/complexes. \n2. Deployment of Security staff of FCI as well as other agencies    like Home Guards, \nDGR sponsored agency & Special Police Officers and  state armed police on need \nbasis. \n3. Security Inspections as well as surprise checks of the depots are conducted from time \nto time at various levels to detect and plug any security lapses.  \n4. Lodging of FIRs with the Police where pilferage and theft has come t"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |484", "t": "Vegetable based Processing Industries", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 484 \nANSWERED ON 19TH DECEMBER, 2017 \n \nVEGETABLE BASED PROCESSING INDUSTRIES  \n \n484.  SHRI VIJAY KUMAR HANSDAK: \n          \nWill the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  Whether the Government has taken any special steps to set up vegetable based processing \nindustries in Sahenganj, Pakur, Godda and Dumka districts of Jharkhand;  \n \n(b)  if not, the reasons therefor; and  \n \n(c) the reaction of the Government on the quality of vegetables being grown in the said districts \nof Jharkhand?   \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) to (c):   Ministry of Food Processing Industries   does not set up any Food Processing \nIndustries in the country on its own. The schemes   implemented by the Ministry of Food Processing \nIndustries for promotion and development of food processing sector in the country are project -\noriented/demand driven ap plicable throughout the country and not state/location specific. The \nschemes provide freedom to the promoters to choose the project locations based on availability of raw \nmaterial, techno-economic feasibility and viability of the project.  \n \n \n***** \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |485", "t": "Revision in Course of Agricultural Education", "head": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 485 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19/12/2017 \n \nREVISION IN COURSE OF AGRICULTURAL EDUCATION \n \n485. DR. RAM SHANKAR KATHERIA: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE    \nकृषि एवं ककसान कल्याण मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether there is a need to revise the courses for agricultural education in an agrarian \ncountry like India; \n \n(b) if so, whether the Government is contemplating to change traditional agricultural edu cation \ncourse for developing 70 percent agricultural area and imparting training to skilled and \nprofessional youths; \n \n(c) whether there is a need to establish new agricultural universities/agricultural colleges and \nagriculture institutes as professional yo uths in agriculture sector are required and if so, the details \nthereof; \n \n(d) whether assistance from international institutes is under consideration for giv ing \ninternational recognition to agricultural education; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृषि एवं ककसान कल्याण मंत्रालय में राज्य मंत्री \n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT) \n \n(a) Yes, Madam. \n  \n(b) The Agriculture course curricula has been revised periodically by Deans’ Committees.  The \nFifth Deans’ Committee Report has been approved in 2016 to bring uniformity and upgradation in \ncurricula to meet the present day demand of higher agricultural education in the country.  The \nrecommendations have been circulated to all Agricultural Universities for implementation.  A copy \nof the said report has also been posted on the website of the ICAR for reference.    \n \n Besides, the Krishi Vigyan Kendras (KVKs) of the country provide non -formal education \nto the farmers by imparting training and skill development.  The contents of such courses are \ndynamic and need based as per different agro -climatic situations.  In additi on to this, the \nAgriculture Skill Council of India (under Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship, \nGovernment of India) has developed some National Skill Qualification Framework aligned courses \n(Qualification Packs) in which skill training is imparted by KVKs to Rural Youth.  \n \n(c) to (e): No, Madam. \n \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |488", "t": "Assistance for Drinking Water", "head": "Page 1 of 2 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING & URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 488 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 19, 2017 \n \nASSISTANCE FOR DRINKING WATER \n \nNo. 488              SH. ANOOP MISHRA: \n   \nWill the Minister of HOUSING & URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the details of the schemes being implemented by the \nGovernment to provide assistance to the States including Madhya \nPradesh for improving the facilities for drinking water;  \n(b) the details of proposals/projects received by the Government \nfrom various States under these schemes since 2014, State-wise; and  \n(c) the number of proposals/projects cleared so far along with \nthe funds sanctioned/released during the above mentioned period, \nState-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) OF THE \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING & URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a). The Ministry of Housing & Urban Affairs, through its scheme \nAtal Mission for Rejuvenation & Urban Transformation (AMRUT), \nprovides assistance to the States / Union Territories for improving \ndrinking water supply in 500 Miss ion cities throughout India. The \n\nPage 2 of 2 \n \nMission was launched on 25 June, 2015, for a period of 5 years from \nFinancial Year 2015-16 to Financial Year 2019-20. \n \n (b) & (c). Under AMRUT, the Government of India does not approve \nindividual projects but accords approval to the State Annual Action \nPlans (SAAPs) only. SAAPs for the entire Mission period have been \napproved by the Ministry for Rs. 77,640 crore in three rounds, viz., \nSAAP-I during 2015-16, SAAP-II duri ng 2016-17 and third and final \nSAAP during 2017-18 out of which Rs. 39,011 crore have been \nprovided for water supply projects in various States/Union Territories \nin the country. The States/Union Territories have been authorized to \napprove individual projects. \n \nThe Government of India does not release its share of funds \nproject wise. Central share is released in three instalments of 20%, \n40% and 40% each for each of the three SAAPs. State-wise details of \nSAAPs, along with details of projects approved in water supply sector \nand funds sanctioned/released since the launch of AMRUT have been \nattached at annexure. \n \n*****\n\nAnnexure page 1 of 2 \n \nAnnexure \n \nAnnexure referred in reply to part (b) & (c) of Lok Sabha Unstarred \nQuestion number 488 for answer on 19 December, 2017 \n \nAll amounts in Rupees crore \nSl. \nNo. \nName of State/ \nUnion Territory \nTotal \nSAAP size \nCommitted \ncentral \nassistance \nProjects \nin Water \nSupply \nsector \nNumber \nof 