atric Scholarship for SC students studying in Class IX and X; \n2. Post-Matric Scholarship for Scheduled Caste Students (PMS-SC); \n3. Scheme for Upgradation of Merit of SC Students; \n4. Pre-Matric scholarship to the children of those engaged in occupations            involving \ncleaning and prone to health hazards ; \n5. Babu Jagjivan Ram Chhatrawas Yojana; \n6. Pradhan Mantri Adarsh Gram Yojana (PMAGY); \n7. Assistance to States/ UTs for Implementation of the Protection of Civil Rights Act, 1955 \nand  SCs and STs (Prevention of Atrocities) Act, 1989; \n8. Pre-Matric Scholarship for the Other Backward Class (OBC) Students; \n9. Post-Matric Scholarship for Other Backward Class Students; \n10. Construction of Hostels for OBC Boys and Girls; \n11.  Dr. Ambedkar  Scheme of Post-Matric Scholarship for students belonging to \nEconomically  Backward Classes (EBCs); and \n12. Dr. Ambedkar  Scheme of  Pre-Matric and Post-Matric Scholarship for students \nbelonging to Denotified  and Nomadic Tribes.  \n  \n\n(b): The review of Centrally Sponsored Schemes relating to the welfare of Scheduled Castes and \nOther Backward Classes is an ongoing process.    \n(c): Except the Scheme of Construction of Hostels for Girls and Boys under Babu Jagjivan Ram \nChhatrawas Yojana, no other Centrally Sponsored Scheme is being implemented through Non -\nGovernmental Organizations.  \n(d): The details of funds sanctioned and relea sed State -wise under Babu Jagjivan Ram \nChhatrawas Yoajan during the last three years is given in Annexure.  \n(e): The monitoring of the Centrally Sponsored Schemes is being done at Central and State level.  \nFurther, Annual Progress Report and Utilization Certificates for the funds released during the \nprevious year(s) are obtained from the State Governments/UTs.  Evalu ation of the schemes is \nconducted by independent agencies from time to time.  \n \n***** \n  \n\nAnnexure \n \nReferred to in part (d) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.509 for 19 -12-\n2017 regarding Centrally Sponsored Scehmes, raised by Shri Harish Chandra \nAlias Harish Dwivedi, MP. \n \nState-wise details of central assistance sanctioned and released to NGOs Under Babu \nJagjivan Ram Chhatrawas Yojna  (BJRCY) during the last three years \n \nFor Girls Hostel \n(Rs. in lakh) \nSl. \nNo. \nName of State 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \nCentral Assistance \nSanctioned Released \n \nSanctioned Released \n \nSanctioned Released \n \n1 Assam - - - 84.80 \n(sanctioned \nduring \n2013-14) \n- - \n2 Bihar - 81.00 \n(sanctioned \nduring \n2013-14) \n- - - - \n3 Haryana 144.00 72.00 809.78 232.28 - 244.17 \n4 Himachal \nPradesh \n- - 292.36 292.36 - - \n5 Maharashtra 338.41 216.45 \n( 45.00 \nlakh \nsanctioned \nduring \n2013-14) \n- 158.40 \n(90.00 \nLakh \nsanctioned \nduring \n2013-14) \n- 90.00 \n(sanctioned \nduring \n2013-14) \n6 Manipur 88.61 44.30 643.56 188.60 \n(44.30 lakh      \n(sanctioned \nduring \n2013-14) \n- 271.54 \n7 Odisha - - 267.46 80.00 - 53.73 \n8 Punjab 281.07 68.68 - 121.47 - 90.90 \n9 Rajasthan 162.92 81.46 - - - - \n10 Tripura - - 315.00 100.00 - 57.50 \n Total 1015.01 563.89 2328.16 1257.91 - 807.84 \n \n \n \n\n \nFor Boys Hostel \n \n(Rs. in lakh) \nSl. \nNo. \nName of State 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \nCentral Assistance  \nSanctioned Released \n \nSanctioned Released \n \nSanctioned Released \n \n  \n      \n1 Punjab - - 46.80 23.40 - - \n2 Maharashtra 120.57 60.28 - 84.59 \n(22.61 \nLakh \nsanctioned \nduring \n2013-14) \n- 65.79 \n3 Himachal Pradesh - - 146.18 86.18 - 60.00 \n4 Rajasthan 90.00 45.00 - - - - \nTotal 210.15 105.28 327.98 194.17 - 125.29 \n \n \n \n-o- \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |51", "t": "National Policy for Women", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *51 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19th December, 2017 \n \nPrice Cap on Medical Devices \n \n*51. DR. KIRIT P. SOLANKI:  \n \n Will the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the decision of the National Pharmaceutical Pricing Authority (NPPA) to put \na price cap on coronary stents has led to an artificial shortage of these stents in the \nmarket;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the steps taken by the Government to address \nthis artificial shortage;  \n \n(c) whether the Government has any proposal to introduce price cap on any other \nmedical device or medicine in the near future; and  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER   IN   THE   MINISTRY   OF   CHEMICALS   &   FERTILIZERS AND \nPARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS (SHRI ANANTH KUMAR) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\nStatement referred to in reply to Lok Sabha Starred Question No. *51 for answer \non 19/12/2017 regarding Price Cap on Medical Devices. \n \n(a) & (b): No such artificial shortage of coronary stents in the country has been \nreported to National Pharmaceutical Pricing Authority (NPPA) after price capping. \n \n(c) & (d): The Government introduces price cap for medicines and medical devices \nwhich are included under Schedule – I of Drugs (Prices Control) Order, 2013 (DPCO, \n2013). At present, there are 845 formulations and 4 medical devices under Schedule  – I \nof DPCO, 2013 for which the Government has fixed the price – caps. Under para 19 of \nDPCO, 2013: ‘Notwithstanding anything contained in this order, the Government may, in \ncase of extra -ordinary circumstances, if it considers necessary so to do in publi c \ninterest, fix the ceiling price or retail price of any Drug for such period, as it may deem \nfit’. \n \n******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |510", "t": "Conviction Rate", "head": "Corrected Reply \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 510 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19th December, 2017. \n \nConviction Rate \n \nSHRIMATI POONAM MAHAJAN: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n (a) whether it is a fact that the crime rate per lakh population has \nincreased and low rate of conviction has been registered in the country \nover the last decade; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof including the reasons for the low rate of \nconviction; \n(c) the number of convictions that has been secured against the cases \nregistered under the Indian Penal Code, 1860 since the year 2005, year \nand State-wise; \n \n(d) whether the Government acknowledges that poor quality of \ninvestigations had led to lower conviction rates; and \n \n(e) if so, the action plan adopted by the Government in order to improve \nthe law and order situation in the country? \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n(a) to (d): The crime rate (number of crime per one lakh of population) for \nthe Indian Penal Code (IPC) crimes increased in 2015 (234.2)  compared \nto the crime rate in 20 06 (167.7) but during 2016, it decreased (233.6) . \n\nThe conviction rate for the IPC Crimes in the country has shown  a mixed \ntrend with the conviction rate of 42.4, 42.9, 42.3, 42.6, 41.7, 40.7, 41.1, \n38.5, 40.2, 45.1, 46.9 and 46.8 during 2005 to 2016 respectively. The \nreasons for low  conviction rate include hostile witnesses,  long duration \nof trails, faulty investigations etc. State/UT-wise Cases Registered, Crime \nRate, Cases Charge Sheeted, Cases Convicted and Conviction Rate under \ntotal Cognizable IPC crimes during 2005 to 2016 is given at Annexure.  \n \n(e): ‘Police’ and ‘Public Order’ are State subjects under the Seventh \nSchedule to the Constitution of India. The responsibilities to maintain law \nand order, protection of life and property rest primarily with the \nrespective State Governments. The State Governmen ts are competent to \ndeal with such offences under the extant provisions of laws . Various \nadvisories to improve law & order situations are issued by Ministry of \nHome Affairs to States/UTs from time to time, which are available in the \nMinistry’s website viz www.mha.gov.in. \n******** \n \n\n                   \nAnnexure \nState/UT wise Cases Registered (CR), Crime Rate, Cases Chargesheeted (CCS), Cases Convicted (COM) and Conviction Rate (CVR) under Total Cognizable Crimes under IPC during 2005 to 2016 \nState \n2005 2006 2007 2008 \nCR Crime Rate CCS CON CVR CR Crime \nRate \nCCS CON CVR CR Crime \nRate \nCCS CON CVR CR Crime \nRate \nCCS CON CVR \nAndhra Pradesh 157123 196.9 125092 30614 37.7 173909 215.4 124089 27712 36.9 175087 214.6 148066 35380 38.0 179275 217.4 148032 31732 38.2 \nArunachal Pradesh 2304 198.8 1463 402 56.9 2294 195.4 1281 384 56.9 2286 192.1 1362 260 59.2 2374 197.2 1367 285 61.4 \nAssam 42006 146.4 22710 3690 18.4 43673 149.8 23460 4751 21.1 45282 152.9 23335 5042 22.6 53333 177.3 23170 2266 16.2 \nBihar 97850 108.9 68729 7258 15.2 100665 110.2 67494 ` 16.0 109420 117.9 81292 8966 16.7 122669 130.1 85495 9981 19.7 \nChhattisgarh 43633 193.2 33170 11085 54.7 45177 196.5 36508 13276 64.0 45845 196.0 35652 14682 56.0 51442 216.4 40235 11945 51.6 \nGoa 2119 140.6 1097 267 27.8 2204 142.1 1238 268 29.4 2479 155.3 1153 285 30.6 2742 166.8 1558 260 26.4 \nGujarat 113414 209.0 86877 16678 30.9 120972 219.6 92019 19883 35.9 123195 220.5 100655 25634 42.3 123808 218.5 98998 25895 38.4 \nHaryana 42664 187.2 28980 10758 38.5 50509 218.1 34313 9582 37.7 51597 219.3 35510 8938 35.6 55344 231.7 37168 14252 42.3 \nHimachal Pradesh 12345 193.5 10140 1793 22.1 13093 203.1 10146 1916 29.1 14222 218.5 10696 1475 25.4 13976 212.7 11066 1875 29.3 \nJammu & Kashmir 20115 176.9 14375 4071 42.1 20787 177.3 15803 4882 43.5 21443 177.2 16792 4346 47.2 20604 164.8 12678 3777 50.9 \nJharkhand 35175 121.8 21107 4061 24.7 36364 124.0 22192 5434 25.5 38489 129.4 24568 8102 24.6 38686 128.3 26156 5898 23.4 \nKarnataka 117580 211.1 80432 22511 30.0 117710 208.9 82890 23548 30.6 120606 211.7 94831 24533 34.8 127540 221.4 113686 