n KPL -\nKamarajar/Ennore Port\nPort trust Kamarajar/Ennore Port Tamil Nadu Under\nlmplementation\nConstruction of ROB - Cochin Port trust Cochin Kerala Under\nlmplementation\n\n29\nlmprovement of exi5ting road\nconnectinS Kolkata Port Trust to\nNH6 and Kolkata Port Trust to\nnearby lndustrial clusters\nMoRTH/NHAI Kolkata Port West Eengal\nDPR to be\nPrepared\n30\nDevelopment of Coastalroad to\nihe Eest of container 'l erminal ll\nat Chennai Port\nM"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |964", "t": "Water Supply Proposals", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES,\nRIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 964\nANSWERED ON 21.12.2017\nWATER SUPPLY PROPOSALS\n964. SHRI BHAGWANTH KHUBA\nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA\nREJUVENATION be pleased to state:\n(a) whether any schemes/proposals for water supply have been submitted to the Government by various\nStates including Karnataka during the last three years;\n(b) if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) the action taken/to be taken by the Government thereon?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA\nREJUVENATION & PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS\n(SHRI ARJUN RAM MEGHWAL)\n(a) to (c) As per the information received from Ministry of Drinking Water and Sanitation (MoDW&S),\nthe State -wise details of the rural drinking water supply project proposals submitted to Department of\nEconomic Affairs, Ministry of Finance through MoDW&S during the last 3 years  are at Annexure-I. In\nrespect of drinking water supply to the border out posts in Border States, six States have submitted\ndrinking water project / proposals in respect of Border out Posts to be funded by Department of Border\nManagement, Ministry of Home Affairs. State wis e details are at Annexure-II. No project proposal has\nbeen submitted by the Government of Karnataka to MoDW&S in the last 3 years. MoDW&S examines\nthe proposals submitted by the State Governments and make suitable recommendations to Department of\nEconomic Affairs for further processing.\nFurther, the Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs (MoH&UA) has launched Atal Mission for\nRejuvenation and Urban Transformation (AMRUT) on 25.06.2015 to strengthen the urban infrastructure\nin the 500 Mission Cities. The Mis sion has first focus on universal coverage of water supply in the\nMission cities. Under AMRUT, the Central Government approves the State Annual Action Plans\n(SAAPs) of States/UTs and releases the funds. The individual projects are prepared, appraised and\nimplemented by the concerned State Government s/UTs in accordance with the Mission Guidelines. The\ndetails of water supply proposals submitted by various States including Karnataka during last three years\nare at Annexure-III. The MoH&UA has approved all the three SAAPs submitted by the Karnataka State\nand released 1st instalment of the approved SAAPs, which is given as under:\n(Rs. in crore)\nFinancial Year 2015-16 2016-17 2017-20 Total\nSAAP Approved 1258.54 1624.72 2069.61 4952.87\nCentral Assistance 592.29 771.60 954.90 2318.79\n1st installment released 118.46 154.32 190.98 463.76\nThe details of schemes/proposals having drinking water supply component submitted by various States /\nUTs to Central Water Commission under Ministry o f Water Resources, River Development and Ganga\nRejuvenation during the last three years are at Annexure-IV.\n*****\n\nAnnexure-I\n(Annexure referred to in reply to Parts (a) to (c) of the Unstarred Question No. 964 to be answered\non 21.12.2017 in the Lok Sabha regarding “Water Supply Proposals”)\nS.No. State Subject Project\nProposal\nCost  (Rs.\nin\nCrores)\n1. West Bengal Project proposal of 4 water supply projects from Govt. of West Bengal\nfor necessary funding through Asian Development Bank (ADB). 2064.37\n2. Maharashtra EAP Proposal of Government of Maharashtra for implementing 6\nnumber Water Supply Projects to cover drinking water supply needs in\nthe villages of districts of Jalna, Chandrapur, Nagpur, Aurangabad and\nParbhani\n596.55\n3. Madhya\nPradesh\nFinancial assistance for Madhya Pradesh Multi Village Rural Water\nSupply Schemes (MVRWSS), from BRICS New Development Bank\n(NDB).\n4500\n4. Madhya\nPradesh\nProposal for Multi Village Rural Water Supply Schemes under JICA -\nODA from Govt. of Japan - reg.\n3000\n5. Madhya\nPradesh\nPreliminary Project Proposal Report ' for financing Multi Village Rural\nWater Supply Schemes (MVRWSS) by the Asian Development Bank\n(ADB).\n4700\n6. Arunachal\nPradesh\nJICA proposal for covering 49 rural administrative headquarters with\ndrinking water and drainage facility\n583.86\n7. Meghalaya Project proposal for providing drinking water and basic sanitation\nfacility to environmentally degraded areas of East Khasi Hills &\nJaintia Hllls District of Meghalaya\n6318.53\n8. Andhra\nPradesh\nDrinking Water Project proposal from Govt. of Andhra Pradesh for\nquality affected habitations in Rayalaseema region and upland and\ntribal areas of coastal region under External Assistance ( AIIB )\n4500\n9. Sikkim Project proposal for providing water supply facility to left out/partially\ncovered habitations in rural areas of South district of Sikkim through\nExternal Assistance (NDB)\n1200\n10. Himachal\nPradesh\nProject proposal for providing water supply facility to left out /\npartially covered habitations in rural areas of Himachal Pradesh and\nwater supply facilities / source augmentation through External\nAssistance (NDB)\n2885.14\n11. Telangana RWSS - Mission Bhagiratha (TDWSP) - Project Proposal from Govt.\nof Telengana for two segm"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |965", "t": "NIFT, Kannur", "head": "LOK SABHA \n  UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 965 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.12.2017 \n \nNIFT KANNUR \n \n965.   SHRIMATI P.K. SREEMATHI TEACHER: \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES            \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether Government has any proposal to start new PG, UG programme/ \ncertificate programmes at National Institute of Fashion Technology (NIFT), Kannur in \nKerala; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes to construct Boys hostel at NIFT, Kannur in \nKerala; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof?  \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र      मंरी (श्री           \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TEXTILES \n(SHRI AJAY TAMTA) \n \n(a) & (b): The following courses are currently being offered by NIFT Kannur : \n \n  Bachelor Progammes – Bachelor of Design (B.Des.) – Design \n 1. Fashion Design (FD) \n 2. Textile Design (TD) \n 3. Knitwear Design (KD) \n 4. Fashion Communication (FC) \n \n  Bachelor Progammes – Bachelor of Fashion Technology (B.F.Tech.) – \nTechnology \n 5. Apparel Production (AP) \n \n  Master Progammes \n 6. Master of Design (M.Des) \n 7. Master of Fashion (M.F.M.) \n \nThere is no proposal to start any new programme at NIFT Kannur. \n \n(c) & (d): As committed by the State Government of Kerala, a hostel for boys was \ninitially provided. However, due to influx of more girl students, the boys’ hostel was \nsubsequently converted to girls hostel. A hostel for boys is in the planning stage.  \n**** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |966", "t": "Attack on Journalists", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.966 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.12.2017 \n \nATTACK ON JOURNALISTS \n \n966.  SHRI  SISIR KUMAR ADHIKARI: \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING  \n          be pleased to state: \n \n(a)   Whether the incidents  of attack on journalists in the country has grown \nup in recent times; \n(b) If so, the number of such incidents reported in the country during the last \nfive years; \n(c) The action taken by the respective State Governments and the support \nextended to the families of the victims; and \n(d) The present status and implementation of Majithia Wage Board \nNotification by the Media Organisation thereof, State-wise ? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND \nBROADCASTING [(COL RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE (Retd.)] \n \n(a) to (c):  State/UT-wise cases registered and persons arrested under attack \non media persons (under section 325,326, 326A and 326B of IPC) during 2014 -\n2016 is enclosed at Annexure-A as National Crime Record Bureau (NCRB) has \nstarted maintaining data in this regard since 2014 only. This data of NCRB \nreveals that there is a substantial decrease in number of cases of attack on \njournalists from 114 in 2014 to 47 in 2016. \nPolice and Public Order are S tate subjects under Seventh Schedule to the \nConstitution of India. The responsibilities to maintain law and order, protection \nof life and property rest primarily with the respective State Government.  \n(d): Statement showing state -wise present status and implem entation of \nMajithia Wage Board Notification by the Newspaper Establishments, is \nannexed as Annexure-B \n***** \n \n \n\nANNEXURE-A \n \nState/UT wise cases registered (CR) and persons arrested (PAR) for attacks on media \npersons (under section 325, 326, 326a & 326bof IPC) during 2014-2016 \nSI.No. State/UT 2014 2015 2016 \nCR PAR CR PAR CR PAR \n1 Andhra Pradesh 4 1 1 0 6 9 \n2 Arunachal \nPradesh 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n3 Assam 2 0 0 0 2 4 \n4 Bihar 22 3 0 0 0 0 \n5 Chhattisgarh 0 0 1 0 0 0 \n6 Goa 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n7 Gujarat 3 4 0 0 1 4 \n8 Haryana 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n9 Himachal \nPradesh 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n10 Jammu & \nKashmir 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n11 Jharkhand 3 0 0 0 0 0 \n12 Karnataka 0 0 0 0 2 0 \n13 Kerala 0 0 0 0 1 1 \n14 Madhya \nPradesh 7 10 19 32 24 14 \n15 Maharashtra 5 6 1 0 0 0 \n16 Manipur 0 0 0 9 0 0 \n17 Meghalaya 1 2 0 0 0 0 \n18 Mizoram 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n19 Nagaland 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n20 Odisha 1 0 0 0 0 0 \n21 Punjab 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n22 Rajasthan 0 0 5 0 0 0 \n23 Sikkim 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n24 Tamil Nadu 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n25 Telangana 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n26 Tripura 2 2 0 0 5 4 \n27 Uttar Pradesh 63 4 1 0 3 3 \n28 Uttarakhand 1 0 0 0 3 2 \n29 West Bengal NR NR NR NR NR NR \n Total (State) 114 32 28 41 47 41 \n30 A & N Islands 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n31 Chandigarh 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n32 D & N Haveli 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n33 Daman & Diu 0 0 0 0 NR NR \n34 Delhi 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n35 Lakshadweep 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n36 Puducherry 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n Total (UTs) 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n Total (All \nIndia) 114 32 28 41 47 41 \nSource : National Crime Records Bureau \nNR: Implies data not received. \n\n \n \nANNEXURE-B \n \nREPORT ON THE IMPLEMENTATION OF THE MAJITHIA WAGE BOARDS \nSl. \nNo\n. \nState/U.T. No. of \nEstablishment \nWhich have \n implemented \nFully Partially Not \n1 2 4 7 8 9 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 14 13 1 - \n2 Assam 52 6 4 40 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 7 1 5 1 \n4 Bihar 44 7 5 32 \n5 Chhattisgarh 78 23 16 34 \n6 Goa 9 2 5 1 \n7 Gujarat 67 4 3 40 \n8 Haryana 12 2 - 10 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 94 5 4 11 \n10 J & K 339 5 3 52 \n11 Jharkhand 154 2 - - \n12 Karnataka 563 3 - 226 \n13 Kerala 22 11 3 2 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 424 6 1 226 \n15 Manipur 15 - 4 11 \n16 Maharashtra 2757 45 23 61 \n17 Meghalaya 22 2 5 12 \n18 Mizoram  - -  \n19 Nagaland 3 1 - - \n20 Odisha 164 37 - 1 \n21 Punjab 531 5 -- 88 \n22 Rajasthan 893 28  2 \n23 Sikkim  - -  \n24 Telangana 20 0 8 1 \n25 Tamil Nadu 82 81 1 - \n26 Tripura 14 - - 14 \n27 Uttar Pradesh 70 24 5 37 \n28 Uttrankhand 8 7 - 1 \n29 West Bengal 33 7 2 13 \n30 A & N Island 32 - -  \n31 Chandigarh UT 8 1 - 7 \n32 Delhi 42 7 5 30 \n33 Daman & Diu  - - - \n34 D & N Haveli  - - - \n35 Lakshadweep  - - - \n36 Puducherry 8 6 - - \n Total 6581 341 103 953 \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |968", "t": "Rain Water Harvesting Structures", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES,\nRIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 968\nANSWERED ON 21.12.2017\nRAIN WATER HARVESTING STRUCTURES\n968. SHRI R. GOPALAKRISHNAN\nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA\nREJUVENATION be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has prepared a Master plan for artificial recharge of groundwater in India;\n(b) if so, the details and the present status thereof;\n(c) whether the Government has identified the locations/areas of the country where the rain water\nharvesting structures are planned to be set up; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof and the time by which the said work is likely to be completed?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA\nREJUVENATION & PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS\n(SHRI ARJUN RAM MEGHWAL)\n(a) to (d) Central Ground Water Board has prepared a conceptual document titled “Master Plan for\nArtificial Recharge to Ground Water” - 2013 which provides information about area specific artificial\nrecharge techniques to augment the ground water resources based on the availability of source water and\ncapability of subsurface formations to accommodate it. The Ma ster Plan envisages construction of about\n1.11 crore artificial recharge structures in urban and rural areas at an estimated cost of about Rs. 79178\ncrore. This comprises around 88 lakh recharge structures/ facilities utilizing rainwater directly from roof\ntop and more than 23 lakh artificial recharge and rainwater harvesting structures for conserving surplus\nrunoff to augment the ground water resources. It is estimated that annually about 85,565 MCM of\nsurplus run-off is to be harnessed to augment the gr ound water. The Master Plan is available in public\ndomain on the website of the Ministry / CGWB and has also been circulated to the State Government s\nfor implementation. State-wise rural and u rban artificial recharge structures as p roposed in the Master\nPlan are given at Annexure. It is also pertinent to mention here that water resources is a State subject\nand thus respective State Government utilize the guidelines issued by the Ministry in their on -going\nschemes for improved ground water resources.\n*****\n\nANNEXURE\nAnnexure referred in reply to Unstarred Q.No. 968 to be answered on 21.12.2017 in\nLok Sabha regarding “Rain Water Harvesting Structures”.\nState Wise Rural and Urban Artificial Recharge Structures as Proposed in Master Plan\nS.No. State Rural Urban Total\nStructure\n(Number)\nCost\n(Rs. in\ncrore)\nStructure\n(Number)\nCost\n(Rs.in\nCrore)\nStructure\n(Number)\nCost\n(Rs. in\ncrore)\n1 Andhra Pradesh\n(undivided)\n68625 2703.15 750000 1230.00 818625 3933.15\n2 Bihar 2058 128.81 100000 164.00 102058 292.81\n3 Chhattisgarh 87202 1856.21 200000 309.00 287202 2165.21\n4 Delhi 8 0.40 142850 1097.50 142858 1097.90\n5 Goa 1393 83.58 10000 16.40 11393 99.98\n6 Gujarat 18775 769.00 500000 725.00 518775 1494.00\n7 Haryana 44727 1255.30 376000 1675.00 420727 2930.30\n8 Himachal 102328 573.47 50000 82.00 152328 655.47\n9 Jammu & Kashmir 1688 168.80 100000 145.00 101688 313.80\n10 Jharkhand 26282 1733.34 200000 290.00 226282 2023.345\n11 Karnataka 72985 1998.66 700000 1148.00 772985 3146.66\n12 Kerala 754427 5457.72 315000 456.75 1069427 5914.47\n13 Madhya Pradesh 532724 10717.61 600000 984.00 1132724 11701.60\n14 Maharashtra 51157 7926.34 1605670 2765.75 1656827 10692.09\n15 NER states 15250 765.90 400000 656.00 415250 1421.90\n16 Odisha 5856 525.00 300000 499.50 305856 1024.50\n17 Punjab 79924 2021.47 375000 1650.00 454924 3671.47\n18 Rajasthan 9603 1566.07 500000 820.00 509603 2386.07\n19 Sikkim 1905 54.07 5000 13.25 6905 67.32\n20 Tamil Nadu 193574 9284.63 0 0.00 193574 9284.63\n21 Uttar Pradesh 108945 7629.28 1200000 1968.00 1308945 9597.28\n22 Uttarakhand 2900 414.75 48250 68.25 51150 483.00\n23 West Bengal 97360 3450.06 300000 522.00 397360 3972.06\n24 A & Nicobar\nIsland\n1640 44.02 1200 8.00 2840 52.02\n25 Chandigarh 0 0.00 8700 652.50 8700 652.50\n26 Dadra & Nagar\nHaveli\n65 3.38 1000 1.50 1065 4.87\n27 Daman and Diu 0 0.00 4044 4.44 4044 4.44\n28 Lakshadweep 0 0.00 6100 30.50 6100 30.50\n29 Puducherry 1740 61.40 1000 3.05 2740 64.45\nTotal 2283141 61192.41 8799814 17985.39 11082955 79177.80"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |969", "t": "Development of Waterproof/Washable Solar Cells", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 969 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.12.2017 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF WATERPROOF/WASHABLE SOLAR CELLS \n \n \n969. SHRI JAYADEV GALLA: \n \n \n Will the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n \n \n(a): whether new type of waterproof/washable solar cells have been developed by the \nUniversity of  Tokyo; and \n(b): if so, the manner in which the above cells are different from the conventional ones being \nused now and the advantages thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR POWER AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY (I/C)  \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a): It has been reported that  waterproof and washable solar cells have been developed by \nresearchers from RIKEN Centre for Emergent Matter Science and University of  Tokyo. \n(b): These are extremely thin and flexible organic solar cells having lower efficiency i.e. ≤ \n8%. These cells made in very small size and of low power, may be integrated with the clothes \nwhich can be worn on the body and can be used to power medical  devices and sensors. On the \nother hand, the conventional solar cells are the silicon wafer -based relatively thick and inflexible \ncells (100-180 microns) and have the efficiencies in the range of 18 -26%. These conventional \ncells are generally used for generation of  electric power. \n \n******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |97", "t": "Vacant Posts of Directors in PSEs and PSBs", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CORPORATE AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 97 \nANSWERED ON FRIDAY, THE 15TH DECEMBER, 2017/ \nAGRAHAYANA 24, 1939 (SAKA) \n \nVACANT POSTS OF DIRECTORS IN PSEs AND PSBs \n \nQUESTION \n \n97. SHRI K.N. RAMACHANDRAN: \n      SHRI BHARATHI MOHAN R. K.: \n      SHRIMATI V. SATHYA BAMA: \n   \nWill the Minister of CORPORATE AFFAIRS                                                कारपोरेट कार्य मंत्री    \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has taken any steps to fill up the vacant Non-Official Part-\ntime (OPTN), women and Independent Directors in various Public Sector Enterprises \n(PSEs) and Public Sector Banks (PSBs); \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the decision taken by the Government in this regard; \n(c)  whether the Government has taken steps to include subject experts and eminent \nprofessionals in the respective fields instead of appointing on political lineage; and   \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and the list of appointments made in the last three years, \nyear/company-wise? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LAW AND JUSTICE              (SHRI P. P. CHAUDHARY) \nAND CORPORATE AFFAIRS                                                                      (��������������������������������)�� \nविवि��और��न्याय��एिं��कारपोरेट��कायय��मंत्रालय��में��राज्य��मंत्री \n���������������������������������������������������� \n(a):- Appointment of Directors/Women Directors in CPSUs and PSBs are made on the \nbasis of prescribed guidelines.  Government has prescribed the time -lines for various \nassociated activities such as furnishing the proposal, processing of proposals for \nobtaining recommendations of Search Committee and processing the \nrecommendations of Search Committee for obtaining the approval of competent \nauthority to ensure timely appointments to the Boards of the CPSUs and PSBs.      \n(b) to (c): - The Government has laid down the criteria in terms of educational \nqualifications, age and experience for persons to be considered for appointment as \nnon-official Directors on the Boards of CPSEs. In te rms of laid down criteria, \nprofessionals of repute having more than 15 years of relevant domain experience in \nfields relevant to the company's area of operation and persons of eminence with proven \ntrack record from Industry, Business, Agriculture or Manage ment are eligible to be \n\nconsidered for such appointments.  For Public Sector Banks also, the guidelines for \nappointment of Non -Official Directors provide for consideration of persons from \namongst the persons of eminence with special academic training or practical \nexperience in relevant fields, chartered accountants, academicians, and retired senior \ngovernment officials.  \n(d):- Details of appointments made during the 3 years are provided at Annexure. \n \n******* \n�\n�\n�\n� �\n\nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN THE REPLY TO LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 97  \n \nYear 2015 \n \nA. CPSEs:- Details of Non-Official Directors  \n \nName of CPSEs Number of non-Official Directors \nAppointed \n BEML Limited 3 \n Bharat Coking Coal Limited 2 \n Bharat Dynamics Limited 2 (1 Women) \n Bharat Electronics Limited 3 (1 Women) \n Bharat Heavy Electricals Limited 3 \n Bharat Petroleum Corporation Limited 3 \n Central Coalfields Limited 2 \n Central Mine Planning & Design Institute Limited 2 \n Coal India Limited 5 (1 Women) \n Eastern Coalfields Limited 1 (1 Women) \n Engineering Projects (India) Limited 2 (1 Women) \n Engineers India Limited 4 (1 Women) \n Ferro Scrap Nigam Limited 1 \n GAIL (India) Limited 3 \n Hindustan Copper Limited 4 (1 Women) \n Hindustan Petroleum Corporation Limited 1 \n Hindustan Shipyard Limited 1 \n Indian Oil Corporation Limited 2 \n Indian Rare Earths Limited 1 (1 Women) \n ITI  Limited 1 \n KIOCL Limited 1 \n Mahanadi Coalfields Limited 2 \n Mahanagar Telephone Nigam Limited 2 \n Mazagon Dock Limited 2 (1 Women) \n MECON Limited 1 \n Mineral Exploration Corpn. Limited 1 (1 Women) \n Mishra Dhatu Nigam Limited 3 (1 Women) \n MOIL Limited 1 (1 Women) \n National Aluminium Company Limited 5 \n Neyveli Lignite Corpn. Limited 3 \n NHPC Limited 4 (1 Women) \n NMDC Limited 6 (1 Women) \n North Eastern Electric Power Corporation Limited 3 \n Northern Coalfields Limited 2 \n NTPC Limited 1 (1 Women) \n Oil & Natural Gas Corporation Limited 3 \nPower Grid Corporation Of India Limited 1 \n Rashtriya Ispat Nigam Limited 4 \n\nRural Electrification Corpn. Limited 4 (1 Women) \n SJVN Limited 4 (1 Women) \n South Eastern Coalfields Limited 2 \n Steel Authority Of India Limited 4 (1 Women) \n Telecommunications  Consultants (India)  Limited 1 (1 Women) \n THDC India Limited 3 \n Western Coalfields Limited 4 \nTotal  113 (19 Women) \n \n \n B. PSBs:- Details of  Non - Executive Chairman / Non - Official Directors  \n \n \nYear 2016 \nA. CPSEs:- Details of Non-Official Directors  \n \nName of CPSEs Number of non-Official Directors \nAppointed \nAndrew Yule & Co. Limited 2 \nBBJ Construction Co. Limited 2 (1 Women) \nBengal Chemicals & Pharmaceuticals Limited 1 \nBharat Electronics L"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |970", "t": "Renewal Coating on NHs in Madhya Pradesh", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 970 \nANSWERED ON 21ST   DECEMBER, 2017 \n \nRENEWAL COATING ON NHS IN MADHYA PRADESH  \n \n970. SHRI B.V. NAIK:  \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  be pleased to state:  \n    lM+d ifjogu vkSj jktekxZ ea=h \n(a) whether the Government has any programme/policy for renewal coating on the National \nHighways in Madhya Pradesh;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the interval at which the renewal coating is required to be done on \nNational Highways in the State; and  \n(c) whether there is any delay in carrying out renewal coating on National Highways in the State \nand if so, by when such coating would be completed? \n \n  ANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) to (c) Yes, Madam. Periodic Renewal is carried out a s per the policy of the Ministry, \ndepending upon the condition of the road, inter se priority and availability of funds. There is no \nsuch delay reported.  \n \n******** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |971", "t": "Development along Ganga River Banks under CSR", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF WATER RESOURCES,\nRIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †971\nANSWERED ON 21.12.2017\nDEVELOPMENT ALONG GANGA RIVER BANKS UNDER CSR\n†971. SHRI SHER SINGH GHUBAYA\nDR. RAVINDRA KUMAR RAY\nWill the Minister of WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND GANGA\nREJUVENATION be pleased to state:\n(a) whether some Indian companies have expressed their willingness for development and providing\nfacilities along the banks of Ganga river with the funds under Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR);\nand\n(b) if so, the names of such companies along with the details of the works for which these companies\nhave expressed their interest?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT AND\nGANGA REJUVENATION & HUMAN RESOURCES DEVELOPMENT\n(DR. SATYA PAL SINGH)\n(a) & (b) Yes, Madam. The following 11 Indian & Foreign business groups/companies/individuals\nhave conveyed their intent to take up certain works mainly relating to river front development,\nconstruction/renovation of ghats, crematoria, providing public amenities and for technological\ncooperation.\ni. Shri Ravi Malhotra, Foresight Shipping.\nii. Shri Anil Agarwal, Vedanta Resources Group\niii. Hinduja’s Group\niv. Shri Prakash Lohia, Indorama Corporation\nv. Shri Shiv Nadar, Chairman HCL\nvi. Celtic Renewables\nvii. Lyndon Water\nviii. Mebifarm\nix. NVH Technologies\nx. Arkatap\nxi. Reliance Industries\n******"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |972", "t": "Spot Power Rates Fluctuation", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.972 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.12.2017 \n \nSPOT POWER RATES FLUCTUATION \n \n972.  SHRI OM BIRLA: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has taken any step for keeping the instances of \nspot power rates fluctuations in check; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(c)  the number of instances in the last three years where such spot power \nrates fluctuations occurred? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER AND \nNEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY  \n \n( SHRI  R. K. SINGH ) \n \n(a) & (b) :  In accordance with the provisions contained in Section 66 of the \nElectricity Act, 2003 and clause 5.7.1(f) of the National Electricity Policy, Central \nElectricity Regulatory Commission (CERC) bro ught out the CERC (Power Market) \nRegulations, 2010. These Regulations provide for power exchanges to function \nwith the objective to ensure fair, neutral, efficient and robust price discovery.  \n \nThe Regulations provides that the bidding mechanism shall be \ndouble sided closed bid auction for price discovery on a day -ahead basis, and the \nprice discovered for the unconstrained market shall be a uniform market clearing \nprice for all buyers and sellers who are cleared. \n \nThere is voluntary participation in the pow er exchanges in India. \nThere are more than 3000 buyers and sellers bidding on the power exchange \nplatform.  \n \n(c) :  Prices on day -ahead market on Indian Energy Exchange (IEX) and \nPower Exchange India Limited (PXIL) fluctuate in every block (15 minutes) lea ding \nto fluctuations in a day depending upon the electricity demand and supply \nposition in the market at any given point in time .  During the period from April, \n2014 to October, 2017, the number of instances of significant fluctuations in \nelectricity price s, i.e. increase in weighted average day -wise prices above the \nweighted average month-wise prices, were about 139 on IEX and 142 on PXIL.   \n \n********* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |973", "t": "Setting up/Upgradation of Akashwani Kendras", "head": "         \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n MINISTRY OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 973 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 21.12.2017) \n \nSETTING UP/UPGRADATION OF AKASHWANI KENDRAS \n973.  SHRI BODHSINGH BHAGAT:  \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether budget allocation has been made for setting up and upgradation of \nsanctioned and proposed Akashwani Kendras in Siwani and Balaghat districts \nin Madhya Pradesh; and  \n(b)  if so, the funds sanctioned and the time by which the work related to setting \nup of the said Akashwani Kendras is likely to be completed? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND \nBROADCASTING  \n{COL RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE (Retd.)} \n \n  \n(a) & (b)  As informed by Prasar Bharati, budgetary allocation of Rs. 22 lakhs for \n100 W FM transmitter for Seoni and Rs.182 lakhs for Balaghat for upgradation of \nFM transmitter from 6 KW to 10 KW has been made for commissioning by \nNovember, 2018 and October, 2018 respectively.  \n****** \n  \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |974", "t": "Speed Breakers", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 974 \n \nANSWERED ON 21STDECEMBER, 2017 \n \nSPEED BREAKERS \n  \n974. SHRI DILIP PATEL: \n \n Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether speed breakers in the country claim more lives than all road accidents; \n(b) if so, the details in this regard; \n(c) whether faulty design, poor material and lack of prominent markings make these \nspeed breakers dangerous for road users; \n(d) if so, the corrective steps taken in this regard; \n(e) whether States have been instructed to ensure proper norms and designate locations \nfor building speed breakers; and \n(f) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \n  \n(SHRI MANSUKH L.MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) & (b) This Ministry is primarily responsible for development & maintenance of \nNational Highways and speed breakers are not allowed on National Highway as they are \none of the causes for accidents. \n(c) to (f) All the implementing agencies namely, National Highways Authority of India  \n(NHAI), State Public Works Departments  (PWDS), National Highways and Infrastructure \nDevelopment Corporation Ltd. (NHIDCL), Border Roads Organization (BRO) and \nHighway Adminis trations have been instructed to ensure that all the speed breakers are \nremoved on National Highways.  The Indian Road Congress (IRC)  guidelines for traffic \ncalming measures in urban and rural areas to control the speed on highway  are applicable \nto National Highways also. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |975", "t": "Stressed Power Plants", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF POWER \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.975 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.12.2017 \n \nSTRESSED POWER PLANTS \n \n975.  SHRI A.T. NANA PATIL: \n \n Will the Minister of POWER \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether NTPC had decided a few years back to acquire certain \nstressed power assets in central as well as State power sectors; \n \n(b)  if so, the details of such assets for which acquisition has been \ncompleted; \n \n(c)  whether the Government has taken any steps for the running of debt \nridden power plants by lenders themselves for a fee instead of acquisition; \nand \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR POWER AND \nNEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY  \n \n( SHRI  R. K. SINGH ) \n \n(a) & (b) :   NTPC has not decided to acquire any stressed power assets \nin Central or State Power sector.  However, based upon its commercial \nassessment, in 2016, NTPC acquired Patratu TPS under Joint Venture \nroute with Jharkhand Bijli Vitran Nigam Limited  (NTPC 74% shareholding) \nand in June 2017, NTPC signed an MoU with Rajasthan Rajya Vidyut \nUtpadan Nigam Ltd. for Acquisition of Chhabra Power Project.  \n(c) & (d) :  There is no such proposal in the Ministry of Power.  \n \n********** \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |977", "t": "Development of Wind Power", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 977 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.12.2017 \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF WIND POWER  \n \n \n \n977. SHRIMATI DARSHANA VIKRAM JARDOSH: \n \n \n Will the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n \n \n(a): whether the Centre for Wind Energy Technology (CWET) has received any proposal \nfrom the Government of Gujarat  for joint research work and further development of wind \npower in the State and if so, the details thereof and the action taken by the Union Government \nthereon; \n \n(b): whether the Union Government is aware of the vast potential of wind power \ngeneration in Gujarat and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c): whether the Union Government has made/intends to make any assessment in this \nregard and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d): the targets set along with the action plan of the Union Government to tap wind power  \nenergy in the country, especially in Gujarat? \n \nANSWER \n \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR POWER AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY (I/C) \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n \n(a): National Institute of Wind Energy (NIWE) (erstwhile C -WET) has inked  a \nMemorandum of  Understanding   for collaborative research in the field of renewable energy \nwith the Gujarat Energy Research & Management Institute (GERMI), which is supported by \nthe Government of Gujarat. \n \n(b): NIWE has estimated the wind power potential of Gujarat at 100 m level as 8 4.431 \nGW.  \n \n(c): The Wind Resource Assessment in Gujarat state has been carried out by installation \nof wind masts ranging from 20 m to 120 m level. As on 31.10.2017, 82 nos. of Wind \nMonitoring Stations were established in Gujarat.  \n \n(d): The Union Government has set a target of reaching 175 GW of renewable power \ncapacity in the country by 2022. Out of this, 60 GW have been targeted to be achieved from \nwind energy by 2022. This will include installations for Wind Energy in Gujarat.  \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |978", "t": "Utilisation of Funds for Differently Abled Persons", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.978 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.12.2017 \n \nUtilisation of Funds for Differently Abled Persons \n \n978.  SHRIMATI APARUPA PODDAR: \n \n Will the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) details of the utilization of funds for differently abled persons under \nthe Khelo India Scheme;  \n(b) whether there has been Sports Infrastructure creation under Khelo \nIndia Scheme, espe cially for the differently abled, if so, the details \nthereof; and  \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS  \n{COL. RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE (RETD.)} \n \n(a)  Rs.83.70 lakh was released in the year 2017 -18 under the Khelo \nIndia Scheme, for conducting various activities like community coaches \ntraining and holding of games at District, State and National level for \ndisabled persons.  \n(b) The revamped Khelo India Scheme which cam e into effect in \nOctober, 2017 consists of twelve verticals including Promotion of Sports \namong persons with disabilities. The relevant provisions are summarized \nbelow:  \n \n\n \n Financial assistance  on case to case basis, keeping in view fund \nstate, will be pro vided to States/UTs and Sports Authority of India ( SAI) \nfor creation of specialist sports infrastructure for persons with \ndisabilities. Funds required for making stadia disabled friendly / barrier \nfree will be accessed from Scheme for Implementation of Per sons with \nDisabilities Act (SIPDA) of Department of Empowerment of Persons with \nDisabilities. The funds will be used for classification of players, \nequipment, training and preparation of teams for Paralympic Games and \ndisciplines and competitions. Funds wi ll be utilized for classification of \nAthletes, training of Indian classifiers, and setting up/supporting \nSpecialised Sports Training Centres for people with disabilities, coaching \ndevelopment, scholarships for coaching diploma both by differently abled \npersons and able -bodied persons seeking coaching for training para -\nathletes, and competitions.  \n \n(c) Does not arise.  \n \n****** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |98", "t": "Clinical Trials for Ayurvedic Medicine", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYURVEDA, YOGA & NATUROPATHY,\nUNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY\n(AYUSH)\nLOK  SABHA\nUNSTARRED  QUESTION NO. 98\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 15TH DECEMBER, 2017\nCLINICAL TRIALS FOR AYURVEDIC MEDICINE\n98. SHRI K. PARASURAMAN:\nWill the Minister of AYURVEDA, YOGA AND NATUROPATHY, UNANI,\nSIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH) be pleased to state:\n(a) the total number of permissions granted by the Government for clinical trials for ayurvedic\nmedicines;\n(b) whether the Government has constituted regulatory board for Indian traditional medicines on\nthe line of Drugs Controller General of India; and\n(c) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken by the Government to regulate production and the\nsale of Indian traditional medicines?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (IC) OF THE MINISTRY OF AYURVEDA,\nYOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY\n(SHRI SHRIPAD YESSO NAIK)\n(a):     The Government has granted permission for conducting clinical trials on ten\nAyurvedic drugs.\n(b):     Ministry of AYUSH has notified the creation of twelve regulatory posts of Drug\nInspectors, Assistant Drug Controllers and Deputy Drug Controllers as a part of the central\nregulatory framework for administering the provisions of Drugs & Cosmetics A ct, 1940 and\nRules thereunder pertaining to Ayurvedic, Siddha, Unani and Homoeopathy drugs.\n(c ):     Recruitment rules for the said regulatory posts have been framed and their approval\nfrom Department of Personnel & Training, Union Public Service Commission and Law\nMinistry is in the process. Meanwhile, additional charge of these posts has been given to the\nrespective Technical Officers of the Ministry of AYUSH till the regular officers are\nappointed. Manufacturing of Ayurvedic, Siddha and Unani medicines for sale is regulated in\nthe country with the exclusive provisions provided in chapter IVA of the Drugs & Cosmetics\nAct, 1940 and in Rules 151 to 169 of the Drugs & Cosmetics Rules, 1945.  These regulatory\nprovisions are framed and amended by the Central Government on the advice of Ayurvedic,\nSiddha and Unani Drugs Technical Advisory Board and are enforced by the State\n\n-2-\nGovernments.  License is mandatory for the commercial manufacturing of Ayurvedic,\nSiddha and Unani medicines and following steps have been taken to regulate their\nproduction and sale-\ni. Pharmacopoeia Commission of Indian Medicine & Homoeopathy and Pharmacopoiea\nCommittees have been set up to lay down the quality standards of Ayurvedic, Siddha,\nUnani and Homoeopathic drugs, which are published in the respective\npharmacopoeias and are mandatory for the manufacturers to follow in the\nmanufacturing of these medicines.\nii. Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP) and requirements for issue of license to\nmanufacture various types of Ayurvedic, Siddha and Unani medicines on the basis of\nevidence of safety and effectiveness are prescribed in the Drugs & Cosmetics Rules,\n1945.\niii. Rules for indicating shelf -life or expiry date on the labels of the ASU&H medicines\nhave be en notified and similarly, a provision to display ‘caution’ for consuming\nmedicines made from potentially hazardous ingredients under medical supervisions is\nmandatory in the Drugs & Cosmetics Rules.\niv. Quality Certification systems for the ASU products have been introduced.\nv. Central and State Drug Testing Laboratories for ASU drugs are in place and 55\nprivate laboratories have been approved under the Drugs & Cosmetics Rules, 1945 for\nthe quality analysis of ASU drugs and raw materials.\nvi. Grant-in- aid is provided through the Centrally Sponsored Scheme of National\nAYUSH Mission for the quality control activities of ASU&H medicines including\nstrengthening of state pharmacies, drug testing laboratories and enforcement\nframework.\n……………….."}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |980", "t": "Replacement of FM at AIR", "head": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION & BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 980 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 21.12.2017) \n \nREPLACEMENT OF FM AT AIR \n980. SHRI LAXMI NARAYAN YADAV:  \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether action has been taken on the proposal for replacement of 6 KW FM \nby the 10 KW FM at All India Radio, Sagar district in Madhya Pradesh during \nthe 12th Five Year Plan period;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c)  the present status of the implementation of the proposal? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND \nBROADCASTING  \n{COL RAJYAVARDHAN RATHORE (Retd.)} \n  \n(a) to (c) Yes, Madam. Prasar Bharati has informed that scheme for replacement of \n6 KW FM by the 10 KW FM at All India Radio, Sagar District in Madhya Pradesh \nwas approved under 12 th Plan. Purchase order for the transmitter has been placed \nand the transmitter has been inspected. It is presently under shipment from the \nforeign port.  \n****** \n  \n\n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |981", "t": "Inland Waterways", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF SHIPPING\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.981\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21St DECEMBER, 2017\nINLAND WATERWAYS\n981. SHRI MAHEISH GIRRI:\nWill the Minister of SHIPPING be pleased to state:\n+d qkdrrr t-ff\n(a) whether inland waterways are governed by multiple authorities such as Central\nlnland Water Transport Corporation Limited (CIWTC Ltd.), port authorities, State\nGovernments and lnland Watemays Authority of lndia;\n(b) if so, the details thereof ;\n(c) whether the Ministry intends to overcome/subsume multiple authorities into one\nand streamline governing mechanism of inland wateruays:\n(d) if so, the details thereof; and\n(e) if not, the action proposed to be taken by the Government to ensure that there is\nno overlapping of jurisdictions and regulatory turf war between these multiple\nagencies that may unnecessarily delay the development projects?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING\n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN)\n(a)&(b):Under the lnland Wateruays Authority of lndia (lWAl) Act, 1985, the lWAl is\nresponsible for the regulation and development of inland waterways, declared as\nNational Waterways as per the National Waterways Act, 2016, for the purposes of\nshipping and navigation and for matters connected therewith.\n(c)to(e):There is no proposal to subsume multiple authorities for skeamlining the\nmechanism governing the development of National Waterways as no maior\nproblems are being faced on this account.\n)i)t***"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |982", "t": "Double-decker Luxury Buses to Reduce Traffic Congestion", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 982 \nANSWERED ON 21ST DECEMBER, 2017 \n \nDOUBLE-DECKER LUXURY BUSES TO REDUCE TRAFFIC CONGESTION \n \n982. P. NAGARAJAN:  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has  identified 75 routes in various States  including Delhi-Agra, \nLucknow Gorakhpur, Vadodara-Mumbai and Bangalore-Mangalore to operate double decker \nluxury buses to reduce traffic congestion, \n \n(b) if so, the names of such routes  identified/selected by the Government to  operate double \ndecker luxury buses, State-wise; and  \n \n(c) the methods adopted to identify traffic congestion on theses routes? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n(SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n(a) Ministry of Road Transport and Highways has instituted a study under World Bank \nTechnical Assistance (WBTA -27) for passenger mobility enhancement. The study has \nidentified 75 routes in various States/STUs.  \n(b) List of 75 routes is enclosed at annexure ‘A’.  \n(c) The study assessed all India traffic survey dat a and toll data covering major corridors in \nthe country to identify these routes with low passenger share of buses  and high overall \npassenger car unit(PCU) count with a greater share of private vehicles as compared to buses.  \n  \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-A: List of 75 Routes \nS# Routes Origin Origin State Destination Destination State \n1 Delhi - Shimla Delhi Delhi Shimla Himachal Pradesh \n2 Shillong - \nGuwahati \nShillong Meghalaya Guwahati Assam \n3 Gwalior-Bhopal Gwalior Madhya \nPradesh \nBhopal Madhya Pradesh \n4 Bhopal-Dewas-\nIndore \nBhopal Madhya \nPradesh \nIndore Madhya Pradesh \n5 Indore-Dhule-\nNasik \nIndore Madhya \nPradesh \nNasik Maharashtra \n6 Amritsar-Bikaner Amritsar Punjab Bikaner Rajasthan \n7 Jodhpur-Jamnagar Jodhpur Rajasthan Jamnagar Gujarat \n8 Bangalore-Nilgiris Bangalore Karnataka Nilgiris Tamil Nadu \n9 Bangalore-Tirupati Bangalore Karnataka Tirupati Andhra Pradesh \n10 Bangalore-\nMangalore \nBangalore Karnataka Mangalore Karnataka \n11 Patiala-Bhatinda Patiala Punjab Bhatinda Punjab \n12 Chennai-Kurnool Chennai Tamil Nadu Kurnool Andhra Pradesh \n13 Chennai-\nTiruchirappalli \nChennai Tamil Nadu Tiruchirappalli Tamil Nadu \n14 Coimbatore-\nTanjore \nCoimbatore Tamil Nadu Tanjore Tamil Nadu \n15 Delhi-Agra-\nLucknow \nDelhi Delhi Lucknow Uttar Pradesh \n16 Dhule-Aurangabad Dhule Maharashtra Aurangabad Maharashtra \n17 Rajkot-Udaipur Rajkot Gujarat Udaipur Rajasthan \n18 Udaipur-Kota Udaipur Rajasthan Kota Rajasthan \n19 Jhansi-Kanpur-\nLucknow \nJhansi Madhya \nPradesh \nLucknow Uttar Pradesh \n20 Lucknow-\nGorakhpur \nLucknow Uttar \nPradesh \nGorakhpur Uttar Pradesh \n21 Gorakhpur-\nMuzaffarpur \nGorakhpur Uttar \nPradesh \nMuzaffarpur Bihar \n22 Patna -\nMuzaffarpur-\nSiliguri \nPatna Bihar Siliguri West Bengal \n23 Siliguri-Guwahati Siliguri West Bengal Guwahati Assam \n24 Aligarh-Kanpur Aligarh Uttar \nPradesh \nKanpur Uttar Pradesh \n25 Nellore-Guntur-\nVijayawada \nNellore Telangana Vijayawada Andhra Pradesh \n26 Vijayawada-\nVisakhapatnam \nVijayawada Andhra \nPradesh \nVisakhapatnam Andhra Pradesh \n27 Visakhapatnam-\nBhubaneshwar \nVisakhapatnam Andhra \nPradesh \nBhubaneshwar Orissa \n28 Cuttack-Kolkata Cuttack Orissa Kolkata West Bengal \n\n29 Delhi-Jaipur Delhi Delhi Jaipur Rajasthan \n30 Jaipur-Bhilwara Jaipur Rajasthan Bhilwara Rajasthan \n31 Bhilwara-Udaipur Bhilwara Rajasthan Udaipur Rajasthan \n32 Udaipur-\nAhmedabad \nUdaipur Rajasthan Ahmedabad Gujarat \n33 Vadodara-Surat-\nMumbai \nVadodara Gujarat Mumbai Maharashtra \n34 Kolkata-Durgapur Kolkata West Bengal Durgapur West Bengal \n35 Dhanbad-Varanasi Dhanbad Jharkhand Varanasi Uttar Pradesh \n36 Allahabad-Kanpur Allahabad Uttar \nPradesh \nKanpur Uttar Pradesh \n37 Kanpur-Mathura Kanpur Uttar \nPradesh \nMathura Uttar Pradesh \n38 Pune-Kolapur Pune Maharashtra Kolapur Maharashtra \n39 Hubli-Davanagere-\nBangalore \nHubli Karnataka Bangalore Karnataka \n40 Bangalore-\nChennai \nKrishnagiri Karnataka Chennai Tamil Nadu \n41 Hyderabad-\nBelgaum \nHyderabad Telangana Belgaum Karnataka \n42 Jaipur-Agra Jaipur Rajasthan Agra Uttar Pradesh \n43 Jaipur-Ujjain Jaipur Rajasthan Kota Rajasthan \n44 Ahmedabad-Ujjain Ahmedabad Gujarat Ujjain Madhya Pradesh \n45 Ujjain-Bhopal Ujjain Madhya \nPradesh \nBhopal Madhya Pradesh \n46 Lucknow-\nAllahabad \nLucknow Uttar \nPradesh \nAllahabad Uttar Pradesh \n47 Chandigarh-Delhi Chandigarh Chandigarh \n(UT) \nDelhi Delhi \n48 Madurai-Kollam Madurai Tamil Nadu Kollam Tamil Nadu \n49 Kolapur-Mangalore Kolapur Maharashtra Mangalore Karnataka \n50 Mangalore-\nKozhikode \nMangalore Karnataka Kozhikode Kerala \n51 Kozhikode-Kochi Kozhikode Kerala Kochi Kerala \n52 Kochi-\nKanyakumari \nKochi Kerala Kanyakumari Tamil Nadu \n53 Nagpur - Raipur Nagpur Madhya \nPradesh \nRaipur Chhatisgarh \n54 Raipur-\nBhubaneswar \nRaipur Chhatisgarh Bhubaneshwar Orissa \n55 Nashik-Pune Nashik Maharashtra Pune Maharashtra \n56 Srinagar-Jalandhar Srinagar Jammu & \n"}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |983", "t": "Driverless Cars", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 983 \nANSWERED ON 21ST DECEMBER, 2017 \n                        \nDRIVERLESS CARS \n \n983. SHRIMATI K. MARAGATHAM: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n \nlM+d ifjogu vkSj jktekxZ ea=h \nbe pleased to state:\n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to allow driverless cars in India, if so, the details thereof \nand if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(b) whether the country suffers a huge shortage of skilled drivers, if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) whether the cab aggregators are taking advantage of this situation, if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a)   There is no such proposal under consideration in the Ministry regarding driverless cars. \n \n(b) and (c)  Sections 3 to 28 in Chapter II of the Motor Vehicles Act, 1988 (MV Act) contain \ndetailed provisions regarding licensing of drivers of motor vehicles. Section 27 of the MV Act \nempowers the Central Government to make rules on matters specified in clauses (a)  to (g) of the \nsection 27. The Central Motor Vehicles Rules, 1989 (CMVRs) in exercise of the powers \nconferred by various sections of the MV Act provides various rules in regard to licensing of \ndrivers, including driving test and driving schools and establi shments. The learners’ licences and \ndriving licences are, however, issued by authorities of the States/UTs. Hence, details of shortage \nare not centrally maintained by this Ministry.  \n \n \n******* "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |984", "t": "Setting up of Battery Storage Plants", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 984 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.12.2017 \n \nSETTING UP OF BATTERY STORAGE PLANTS \n \n984. PROF. SADHU SINGH: \n \n Will the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n \n(a): whether the Government/National Thermal Power Corporation Limited (NTPC) \nproposes to set up battery storage plants in Andaman and Nicobar Islands; \n \n(b): if so, the details and the benefits thereof;  \n \n(c): the sites identified for the said purpose; and \n \n(d): the total capacity of the said plants and the expenditure likely to be incurred \nthereon? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR POWER AND NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY (I/C) \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n \n(a) to (d): Andaman & Nicobar Islands Administration has requested NTPC to set up a \n2 Megawatt hour (MWh) battery storage system at existing 5 MW Solar PV project of \nNTPC at Garacharma, Port Blair in Andaman & Nico bar Islands. The storage system is \naimed at smoothening the intermittencies in solar power output due to cloud effect. The cost \nof the system will be determined through bidding process. \n \n******* \n "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |986", "t": "Toll Collection Centres", "head": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 986 \nANSWERED ON 21ST DECEMBER, 2017 \n \nTOLL COLLECTION CENTRES \n \n986.  SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH JOSHI: \n ADV. NARENDRA KESHAV SAWAIKAR: \n SHRI SUMAN BALKA: \n  \n Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nlM+d ifjogu vkSj jktekxZ ea=h \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether  the  Government  is  still collecting  toll  on  certain  portions  of National Highways even \nafter ten years of their completion; \n(b) if so, the details of such toll plazas on various National Highways and the toll collected from each \nsuch toll gate during that period along with the minimum distance prescribed between two plazas on \nthe same route; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to stop toll co llection from the toll gates which are in operation \nfor the last ten or more years; \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n(e) the details of funds collected and the manner in which they are distributed between the private \noperators and public entity and utilized by the Government? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n \n \n(a) &(b) The fee collection at NH Fee plazas is done in perpetuity in accordance with the provisions of \nthe National Highways Fee Rules. \n The extant NH Fee Rules 2008 provides that a ny other fee plaza on the same section of \nNational Highway and in the same direction shall not be established within  a distance of sixty \nkilometers p rovided that wh ere the executing authority deems necessary, it may for reasons to be \nrecorded in writing, establish or allow the concessionaire to establish another fee plaza within a \ndistance of sixty kilometers . F urther, a fee plaza may be established within a distance  of sixty \nkilometres from another fee plaza if such fee plaza is for collection of fee for a permanent bridge, \nbypass or tunnel. \n \n\n \n(c) &(d) No, Madam. However, NH fee rules provides for reduction of fee rate to 40% after recovery \nof capital cost in case of  public funded project and after completion of concession period in case of \nPPP project. \n(e) In case of PPP project, the user fee is collected and retained by the concessionaire in accordance \nwith the provisions of the respective Concession A greement(s) and is shared with the Government in \nthe form of premium, in case of BOT(Toll) premium projects.  In case of Public Funded Project, the \nuser fee is collected by the contractor engaged by NHAI and the agreed amount as per the contract is \ndeposited with the NHAI which subsequently gets credited in Consolidated Fund of India (CFI). \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |987", "t": "Four Laning of Road", "head": "  \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 987 \nANSWERED ON 21ST DECEMBER, 2017 \n \nFOUR LANING OF ROAD \n \n987.  SHRI RAM TAHAL CHOUDHARY: \n  \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसडक़ परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the prescribed time limit for the completion of four laning of road between Ranchi to \nTatanagar;  \n \n(b) the reasons for the delay of the above said work; and  \n \n(c) the steps taken by the Government to get the above said work completed at the earliest \nand the outcome thereof? \n \n  ANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) The scheduled completion period for 4 -laning of Ranchi to Tatanagar  stretch of NH -33 (length \n163.500 km) was 912 days from appointed dated i.e. 04-12-2012. \n \n(b) There has been delay in completion of project due to delay in acquisition of land as well as \nfinancial issues with the concessionaire. At present, the physical progress is 46.60%. \n \n(c) Recently, NHAI Board has accorded approval for one time fund infusion in the project subject to \nachievement of 50% physical progress. \n \n***** "}, {"k": "questions|ls|16|13|UNSTARRED |988", "t": "Driving Training Institute", "head": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 998 \nANSWERED ON 21st DECEMBER, 2017 \nNHs IN ASSAM \n998. SHRI SIRAJUDDIN AJMAL: \n \n       Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the details of roads sanctioned under National Highways (NHs) in Assam during each of the \nlast three years and the current year;  \n(b) the details of funds allocated for construction of NHs during each of the last three years and \nthe current year, State-wise including Assam; \n(c) whether the funds have been released for highways in Assam out of the sanctioned amount \nand if so, the details thereof, during the last three years, year-wise; and  \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor and the details of reasons for delay in disbursement of funds? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS  \n (SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n \n(a) During the last three years and the  current year works for the development of National \nHighways (NHs) have been sanctioned by the Ministry covering a length of 824km at a \ncost of Rs.13,411 Crore. The details are at Annexure – I. \n(b) Details of funds allocated for the construction of NHs during the each of the last three \nyears and th e current year  are furnished State -wise including the State of Assam at \nAnnexure - II \n(c) Yes Madam, funds for development of highways in Assam have been released based on \nexpenditure & bills raised by the State Govt.  The details of funds released during the last \nthree years, year-wise are furnished at Annexure –III. \n(d) Does not arise. \n\nANNEXURE-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO.998 \nANSWERED  ON 21.12.2017 ASKED BY SHRI SIRAJUDDIN AJMAL REGARDING NHs IN ASSAM. \n \nSchemes/ \nProgramme  \n2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 2017-18 Total \nLength \n(km) \nAmount \n(Rs. \nCrore) \nLength \n(km) \nAmount \n(Rs. \nCrore) \nLength \n(km) \nAmount \n(Rs. \nCrore) \nLength \n(km) \nAmount \n(Rs. \nCrore) \nLength \n(km) \nAmount \n(Rs. \nCrore) \nNH (O) 73 478 107 199 220 771 15 59 416 1507 \nSARDP-NE 281 8116 83 1871 19 1099 0 0 